OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-finite-entropy-system-without-a-smooth-positive-volume-model-September-25-2026", "title": "A zero-entropy system without a smooth positive-volume model", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-finite-entropy-system-without-a-smooth-positive-volume-model-September-25-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "152", "family_title": "Zero entropy does not guarantee a smooth positive-volume model", "abstract_html": "We construct an ergodic invertible transformation of a standard nonatomic probability space with zero Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy that has no smooth positive-volume model. More precisely, it is not measurably conjugate to any <i>C</i><sup>∞</sup> diffeomorphism preserving a strictly positive smooth probability density on a compact finite-dimensional manifold. A single example excludes every finite dimension, including models on nonorientable manifolds and manifolds with smooth boundary.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A standard nonatomic probability space is a probability space isomorphic, modulo null sets, to the unit interval with Lebesgue probability.</p><p class=\"li\">A smooth positive-volume model of an invertible probability-preserving transformation \\((X,\\mu,T)\\) consists of a compact smooth manifold \\(M\\) of finite dimension, a \\(C^\\infty\\) diffeomorphism \\(S:M\\to M\\), and an \\(S\\)-invariant probability measure \\(\\nu\\) having strictly positive smooth local densities\n  \\[\n  d\\nu=f(x)|dx_1\\cdots dx_d|,\\qquad f&gt;0,\n  \\]\n  together with a measure-preserving isomorphism \\(F:X_0\\to M_0\\) on invariant conull measurable sets such that \\(F\\circ T=S\\circ F\\). Both \\(F\\) and \\(F^{-1}\\) are measurable. The manifold may have smooth boundary, with the densities smooth up to the boundary, and need not be orientable.</p><p class=\"li\">Throughout, logarithms in entropy and combinatorial estimates are to base \\(2\\); natural logarithms are denoted by \\(\\ln\\). For a finite probability vector \\(p\\),\n  \\[\n  H(p)=-\\sum_a p(a)\\log_2p(a),\\qquad 0\\log_2 0=0.\n  \\]\n  For a finite measurable partition \\(\\mathcal P\\), \\(H_\\mu(\\mathcal P)\\) is the entropy of its atom probabilities, and\n  \\[\n  h_\\mu(T)=\\sup_{\\mathcal P}\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac1n\n  H_\\mu\\!\\left(\\bigvee_{j=0}^{n-1}T^{-j}\\mathcal P\\right).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For finite laws \\(p,q\\),\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{TV}(p,q)=\\frac12\\sum_a|p(a)-q(a)|.\n  \\]\n  Write \\(\\mathcal L(Y)\\) for the law of \\(Y\\), and\n  \\[\n  H_{\\mathrm{bin}}(s)=-s\\log_2s-(1-s)\\log_2(1-s).\n  \\]\n  Empty determinants and empty products equal \\(1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exists an ergodic invertible measure-preserving transformation \\((X,\\mu,T)\\) of a standard nonatomic probability space such that \\(h_\\mu(T)=0\\) and \\((X,\\mu,T)\\) is not measurably conjugate, on invariant conull sets, to any \\(C^\\infty\\) diffeomorphism of a compact finite-dimensional manifold preserving a strictly positive smooth probability density. The manifold may have smooth boundary and need not be orientable.</p>", "proof": "<p>Propositions 6–9 construct a nonempty compact phase extension carrying an ergodic invariant probability \\(\\mu\\). Proposition 9 proves that \\((X,\\mu)\\) is standard and nonatomic, that \\(T\\) is invertible and measure preserving, and that\n\\[\nh_\\mu(T)=0.\n\\]</p><p>Proposition 14 excludes every smooth positive-volume model of this fixed system. Its switching input, Proposition 5, allows nonorientable manifolds and smooth boundary. The symbolic construction is fixed first; the hypothetical model, its finite dimension, and all smooth constants are fixed only afterward. Proposition 13 absorbs those constants. Thus this is one example excluding all finite dimensions, not a different counterexample for each dimension.</p><p>The obstruction uses the controlled unnormalized output law of the smooth switch. It makes no analogous assertion for models carrying only a singular invariant measure or a nonsmooth density equivalent to volume.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Proposition 2.2, “Good finite orbit segments.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a compact smooth manifold \\(M\\) of dimension \\(d\\ge1\\), a smooth Riemannian metric, a smooth diffeomorphism \\(S:M\\to M\\), and an \\(S\\)-invariant probability measure \\(\\nu\\). A smooth positive density is not assumed here. Smooth boundary is allowed.</p><p class=\"li\">The orthonormal frame bundle \\(\\mathcal FM\\) consists of the linear isometries \\(e:\\mathbb R^d\\to T_xM\\), with projection \\(\\pi(e)=x\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define the frame map \\(\\widetilde S\\) and the positive-diagonal upper-triangular matrix \\(R(e)\\) by the unique QR factorization\n  \\[\n  DS_x\\circ e=\\widetilde S(e)R(e).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\widetilde\\nu\\) be a \\(\\widetilde S\\)-invariant probability projecting to \\(\\nu\\), whose existence is established in the proof.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), there are a positive sequence \\((b_k)_{k\\ge1}\\) and compact sets \\(\\widetilde G_k\\subset\\mathcal FM\\), \\(G_k=\\pi(\\widetilde G_k)\\subset M\\), such that\n\\[\nb_k\\ge2^{-k/10},\\qquad \\sum_{k\\ge1}b_k^2&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\widetilde\\nu(\\widetilde G_k)\\ge1-\\eta/100,\\qquad\n\\nu(G_k)\\ge1-\\eta/100.\n\\]\nFor \\(n=2^k\\), \\(b=b_k\\), and \\(e\\in\\widetilde G_k\\), put\n\\[\nA_t=R(\\widetilde S^te),\\quad -n\\le t&lt;n,\\qquad\n\\lambda_j=\\frac1{2n}\\sum_{t=-n}^{n-1}\\ln(A_t)_{jj}.\n\\]\nThen, for every \\(j\\) and every integer \\(-n\\le u\\le n\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\\sum_{t=-n}^{u-1}\\ln(A_t)_{jj}-(u+n)\\lambda_j\\right|\\le bn.\n\\]\nConsequently, for every \\(-n\\le u\\le v\\le n\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\\sum_{t=u}^{v-1}\\ln(A_t)_{jj}-(v-u)\\lambda_j\\right|\\le2bn.\n\\]</p><p>For subsequent smooth estimates, fix \\(\\eta\\) and use\n\\[\nb=b_k,\\qquad B=b^{1/8},\\qquad g=b^{1/2},\\qquad\nL=\\lceil b^{1/4}n\\rceil,\n\\]\n\\[\nh_0=e^{-Bn},\\qquad r=e^{-2Bn},\\qquad \\delta=e^{-3Bn/2}.\n\\]\nThese parameters satisfy\n\\[\n\\ln n=o(bn),\\quad bn=o(gL),\\quad gL=o(gn),\\quad\ngn=o(L),\\quad L=o(Bn),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_kB_k^{16}=\\sum_kb_k^2&lt;\\infty,\\qquad Bn\\ge n^{79/80}.\n\\]\nA frame in \\(\\widetilde G_k\\) is called good at scale \\(k\\); a point of \\(G_k\\) is a good base point and has a good witness frame.</p>", "proof": "<p>The positive-diagonal QR factorization is ordinary Gram–Schmidt in the indicated order, choosing the length of each new perpendicular component positive. It depends smoothly on the frame and base point.</p><p>The map \\(\\widetilde S\\) is invertible. If \\(DS_xe=e'R\\), then\n\\[\n(DS_x)^{-1}e'=eR^{-1}.\n\\]\nThe matrix \\(R^{-1}\\) is upper triangular with positive diagonal, so taking positive-diagonal QR after the inverse derivative recovers \\(e\\). This also proves continuity of the inverse. The construction uses the full orthonormal frame bundle and requires neither a global frame nor an orientation.</p><p>Put normalized orthogonal-group Haar measure on each fiber over \\(\\nu\\). Orthogonal changes of local trivialization preserve this measure. Every iterate under \\(\\widetilde S\\) projects to \\(\\nu\\). Since \\(\\mathcal FM\\) is compact, Cesàro averages have a weakly convergent subsequence. The difference between the pushforward of an average and the average itself tends to zero against every continuous function, so the limit \\(\\widetilde\\nu\\) is invariant. Continuity of \\(\\pi\\) preserves its marginal \\(\\nu\\).</p><p>Compactness and invertibility give \\(A_*&gt;1\\) such that\n\\[\n\\|R(e)\\|,\\ \\|R(e)^{-1}\\|\\le A_*,\\qquad e\\in\\mathcal FM.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\ng_j(e)=\\ln R(e)_{jj},\\qquad 1\\le j\\le d,\n\\]\nare continuous and bounded, and \\((g_j(\\widetilde S^te))_{t\\in\\mathbb Z}\\) is stationary under \\(\\widetilde\\nu\\).</p><p>Apply Lemma 1.1 to each \\(g_j\\). Let \\(c_{j,h}\\) be the right side of its estimate for \\(\\|Z_h\\|_{L^2}\\), and put\n\\[\n\\alpha_h^2=\\sum_{j=1}^dc_{j,h}^2.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\sum_h\\alpha_h^2&lt;\\infty\\). Take\n\\[\nb_k=40\\eta^{-1/2}\\alpha_{k+1}+2^{-k/10}.\n\\]\nThis sequence has the required lower bound and square summability.</p><p>The interval \\([-n,n)\\) has length \\(2^{k+1}\\). Stationarity and the squared Markov inequality give\n\\[\n\\widetilde\\nu\\!\\left(\n\\max_{\\substack{1\\le j\\le d\\\\-n\\le u\\le n}}\n\\left|\\sum_{t=-n}^{u-1}g_j(\\widetilde S^te)-(u+n)\\lambda_j\\right|\n&gt;b_kn\n\\right)\n\\le\\frac{4\\alpha_{k+1}^2}{b_k^2}\\le\\frac{\\eta}{400}.\n\\]\nDefine \\(\\widetilde G_k\\) by the complementary non-strict inequalities. These are finitely many continuous inequalities on a compact bundle, so \\(\\widetilde G_k\\) and its projection are compact. Since\n\\[\n\\pi^{-1}(M\\setminus G_k)\\subset\\mathcal FM\\setminus\\widetilde G_k,\n\\]\nthe pushforward identity \\(\\pi_*\\widetilde\\nu=\\nu\\) proves the base-measure estimate. No measurable choice of a frame over every point of \\(G_k\\) is needed.</p><p>Subtracting the chord errors at \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) proves the all-subinterval estimate. Finally, \\(b_k\\to0\\) and \\(bn\\ge n^{9/10}\\) imply the displayed scale comparisons. The ceiling in \\(L\\) has no effect on them, and \\(B^{16}=b^2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1, “Dyadic chord estimate.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\((Y_t)_{t\\in\\mathbb Z}\\) be a real stationary process with \\(\\mathbb EY_0^2&lt;\\infty\\). For \\(h\\ge0\\), define\n\\[\nA_h=2^{-h}\\sum_{t=0}^{2^h-1}Y_t,\\qquad\nZ_h=2^{-h}\\max_{0\\le u\\le2^h}\n\\left|\\sum_{t=0}^{u-1}Y_t-uA_h\\right|,\n\\]\nwhere the maximum is over integer \\(u\\). Set\n\\[\nv_l=\\mathbb EA_{l-1}^2-\\mathbb EA_l^2,\\qquad l\\ge1.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Then \\(v_l\\ge0\\), \\(\\sum_{l\\ge1}v_l\\le\\mathbb EY_0^2\\), and\n\\[\n\\|Z_h\\|_{L^2}\\le\\frac12\\sum_{a=0}^{h-1}2^{-a/2}\\sqrt{v_{h-a}},\n\\qquad h\\ge1.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\sum_{h\\ge1}\\|Z_h\\|_{L^2}^2\\le C\\mathbb EY_0^2\n\\]\nfor an absolute constant \\(C\\). The same estimates hold for a block beginning at any deterministic integer time.</p>", "proof": "<p>Consider a block of length \\(2^l\\), and let \\(U,V\\) be the averages on its left and right halves. Its average is \\(W=(U+V)/2\\). The identity\n\\[\n\\frac{(U-W)^2+(V-W)^2}{2}\n=\\frac{U^2+V^2}{2}-W^2\n\\]\nand stationarity give\n\\[\nv_l=\\mathbb E\\frac{(U-W)^2+(V-W)^2}{2}\n=\\mathbb E(U-W)^2.\n\\]\nThe last equality also follows pointwise from \\(V-W=-(U-W)\\). Thus \\(v_l\\ge0\\), and telescoping proves the bound on their sum. No independence of the two halves is used.</p><p>For a block of length \\(2^h\\), let \\(F_a\\), \\(0\\le a\\le h\\), be the piecewise-linear interpolant agreeing with the cumulative sum at multiples of \\(2^{h-a}\\). Then \\(F_0\\) is the chord and \\(F_h\\) agrees with every integer-time cumulative sum. Passing from \\(F_a\\) to \\(F_{a+1}\\) inserts the midpoint of each of \\(2^a\\) intervals of length \\(2^{h-a}\\). If \\(U_j,W_j\\) are the left-child and parent averages on the \\(j\\)-th interval, its midpoint displacement is \\(2^{h-a-1}(U_j-W_j)\\). Therefore\n\\[\n2^{-h}\\|F_{a+1}-F_a\\|_\\infty\n\\le2^{-a-1}\\max_{1\\le j\\le2^a}|U_j-W_j|.\n\\]\nBounding a squared maximum by the sum of squares and using stationarity gives\n\\[\n\\left\\|2^{-h}\\|F_{a+1}-F_a\\|_\\infty\\right\\|_{L^2}\n\\le2^{-a-1}(2^av_{h-a})^{1/2}.\n\\]\nThe triangle inequality, first for the polygonal sup norm and then in \\(L^2\\), proves the asserted estimate for \\(Z_h\\).</p><p>Extend \\((\\sqrt{v_l})_{l\\ge1}\\) by zero to nonpositive indices. Its squared \\(\\ell^2\\) norm is at most \\(\\mathbb EY_0^2\\). The right side of the estimate is its convolution with the summable sequence \\((2^{-a/2-1})_{a\\ge0}\\). The triangle inequality for the \\(\\ell^2\\) norms of its translates proves the square-summability assertion. Stationarity identifies the distribution of each translated block with the block just considered.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 3.1, “Finite-interval splitting.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(d\\ge1\\), \\(A_*&gt;1\\), \\(n\\ge2\\), and \\(b&gt;0\\), with \\(\\ln(2n+1)\\le bn\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A_t\\), \\(-n\\le t&lt;n\\), be upper-triangular invertible real \\(d\\times d\\) matrices with positive diagonal such that\n  \\[\n  \\|A_t\\|,\\ \\|A_t^{-1}\\|\\le A_*.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\lambda_j=\\frac1{2n}\\sum_{t=-n}^{n-1}\\ln(A_t)_{jj},\n  \\]\n  and assume\n  \\[\n  \\left|\\sum_{t=-n}^{u-1}\\ln(A_t)_{jj}-(u+n)\\lambda_j\\right|\\le bn\n  \\]\n  for all \\(-n\\le u\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le j\\le d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(t&gt;s\\), write\n  \\[\n  A(t,s)=A_{t-1}\\cdots A_s,\\quad A(s,s)=\\operatorname{Id},\\quad\n  A(s,t)=A(t,s)^{-1}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">All norms are Euclidean operator norms. For a \\(q\\)-dimensional subspace \\(E\\) and an injective linear map \\(A\\), define\n  \\[\n  J_q(A|E)=\\|(\\bigwedge^qA)\\xi\\|,\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\xi\\) is either unit simple \\(q\\)-vector spanning \\(E\\). Set \\(J_0=1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(C=C(d,A_*)\\) with the following properties.</p><p>First, if \\(\\lambda_{(1)}\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\lambda_{(d)}\\) are the ordered rates, then for every \\(0\\le q\\le d\\) and \\(-n\\le s\\le t\\le n\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\\ln\\|\\bigwedge^qA(t,s)\\|-(t-s)\\sum_{j=1}^q\\lambda_{(j)}\\right|\\le Cbn.\n\\]\nThe analogous estimate for \\(A(s,t)\\) uses the \\(q\\) largest members of \\(\\{-\\lambda_1,\\ldots,-\\lambda_d\\}\\).</p><p>Next, suppose \\(\\gamma\\in\\mathbb R\\) and \\(g&gt;0\\) separate the rates: every \\(\\lambda_j\\) is at most \\(\\gamma-g\\) or at least \\(\\gamma+g\\). Define\n\\[\nF=\\{j:\\lambda_j\\ge\\gamma+g\\},\\qquad p=|F|,\\qquad\n\\Lambda=\\sum_{j\\in F}\\lambda_j,\\qquad\n\\Omega=-\\sum_{j\\notin F}\\lambda_j.\n\\]\nThere are decompositions\n\\[\n\\mathbb R^d=E_t^s\\oplus E_t^f,\\qquad -n\\le t\\le n,\n\\]\ninvariant under \\(A_t\\), with \\(\\dim E_t^f=p\\), whose complementary projections have norm at most \\(e^{Cbn}\\). For \\(s\\le t\\), \\(l=t-s\\), they satisfy\n\\[\n\\|A(t,s)|E_s^s\\|\\le e^{(\\gamma-g)l+Cbn},\\qquad\n\\|A(s,t)|E_t^f\\|\\le e^{-(\\gamma+g)l+Cbn},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|\\bigwedge^pA(t,s)\\|\\le e^{\\Lambda l+Cbn},\\qquad\ne^{\\Lambda l-Cbn}\\le J_p(A(t,s)|E_s^f)\\le e^{\\Lambda l+Cbn},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\|\\bigwedge^{d-p}A(s,t)\\|\\le e^{\\Omega l+Cbn},\\qquad\ne^{\\Omega l-Cbn}\\le J_{d-p}(A(s,t)|E_t^s)\\le e^{\\Omega l+Cbn}.\n\\]\nIf \\(0&lt;p&lt;d\\) and at least one of \\(v_1,\\ldots,v_p\\) belongs to \\(E_s^s\\), then\n\\[\n\\|A(t,s)v_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge A(t,s)v_p\\|\n\\le e^{(\\Lambda-2g)l+Cbn}\\prod_{j=1}^p\\|v_j\\|.\n\\]\nThe backward analogue has degree \\(d-p\\), exponent \\((\\Omega-2g)l+Cbn\\), and at least one factor in \\(E_t^f\\). Empty-group statements use the zero-space and exterior-degree-zero conventions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Define\n\\[\ne_j(u)=\\sum_{t=-n}^{u-1}\\bigl(\\ln(A_t)_{jj}-\\lambda_j\\bigr),\\qquad\nD_u=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{e_1(u)},\\ldots,e^{e_d(u)}).\n\\]\nThe chord hypothesis gives \\(\\|D_u\\|,\\|D_u^{-1}\\|\\le e^{bn}\\). The matrices\n\\[\n\\widehat A_t=D_{t+1}^{-1}A_tD_t\n\\]\nare upper triangular with constant diagonal \\((e^{\\lambda_1},\\ldots,e^{\\lambda_d})\\) and entries bounded by \\(e^{Cbn}\\). Since the rates are averages of logarithms of diagonal entries,\n\\[\n|\\lambda_j|\\le\\ln A_*.\n\\]\nTheir inverse matrices also have entries bounded by \\(e^{Cbn}\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(m\\), consider upper-triangular \\(m\\times m\\) matrices with constant diagonal \\(e^{\\alpha_1},\\ldots,e^{\\alpha_m}\\), bounded \\(|\\alpha_i|\\), and off-diagonal entries bounded by \\(e^{Cbn}\\). A term in any product entry can change its coordinate index strictly at most \\(m-1\\) times. There are at most a fixed constant times \\((2n+1)^{m-1}\\) choices for these times and intermediate indices. For a product of length \\(l\\), a term with \\(a\\le m-1\\) strict changes has diagonal contribution at most \\(e^{\\alpha_{\\max}(l-a)}\\). The omitted diagonal factors cost at most \\(e^{a|\\alpha_{\\max}|}\\) after factoring out \\(e^{\\alpha_{\\max}l}\\), and the off-diagonal factors contribute \\(e^{C_mbn}\\). The polynomial number of terms is absorbed by \\(\\ln(2n+1)\\le bn\\). Thus the product norm is at most \\(e^{\\alpha_{\\max}l+C_mbn}\\).</p><p>Apply this to \\(\\bigwedge^q\\widehat A_t\\) in the lexicographically ordered coordinate-wedge basis. Its diagonal entries are\n\\[\n\\exp\\!\\left(\\sum_{j\\in I}\\lambda_j\\right),\\qquad |I|=q,\n\\]\nand the largest rate sum is \\(\\sum_{j=1}^q\\lambda_{(j)}\\). For a maximizing set \\(I\\), the corresponding diagonal entry of the product is exactly\n\\[\n\\exp\\!\\left(l\\sum_{j\\in I}\\lambda_j\\right),\n\\]\nwhich is no larger than the operator norm. Conversion back costs only the endpoint gauge bounds. This proves the first assertion. Applying the same argument to inverse normalized steps in reverse time proves the backward assertion. Degrees \\(0\\) and \\(d\\) give the identity and determinant line.</p><p>To separate the two groups, process superdiagonals in increasing order of \\(j-i\\). At a position \\(i&lt;j\\) in different groups, let its current entry be \\(h_t\\). An elementary change\n\\[\nQ_t=\\operatorname{Id}+u_tE_{ij}\n\\]\nchanges that entry in \\(Q_{t+1}^{-1}\\widehat A_tQ_t\\) to\n\\[\nh_t+e^{\\lambda_i}u_t-e^{\\lambda_j}u_{t+1}.\n\\]\nSet it to zero by solving\n\\[\nu_{t+1}=qu_t+f_t,\\qquad\nq=e^{\\lambda_i-\\lambda_j},\\qquad f_t=e^{-\\lambda_j}h_t.\n\\]\nIf \\(q&lt;1\\), prescribe \\(u_{-n}=0\\) and use\n\\[\nu_t=\\sum_{a=-n}^{t-1}q^{t-1-a}f_a.\n\\]\nIf \\(q&gt;1\\), prescribe \\(u_n=0\\) and use\n\\[\nu_t=-\\sum_{a=t}^{n-1}q^{t-1-a}f_a.\n\\]\nIn both formulas coefficients have modulus at most one, so\n\\[\n|u_t|\\le2n\\max_a|f_a|.\n\\]\nDifferent groups cannot have \\(q=1\\), and no division by a possibly small rate separation is used.</p><p>Right multiplication changes column \\(j\\) by a multiple of column \\(i\\); left multiplication changes row \\(i\\) by a multiple of row \\(j\\). Upper triangularity shows that every affected off-diagonal position has distance at least \\(j-i\\), and at that distance only \\((i,j)\\) changes. The quadratic cross term vanishes because\n\\[\nE_{ij}\\widehat A_tE_{ij}=(\\widehat A_t)_{ji}E_{ij}=0.\n\\]\nLater operations therefore cannot restore entries on earlier superdiagonals.</p><p>There are at most \\(d(d-1)/2\\) operations. At each operation the shear and inverse shear are bounded by \\(e^{Cbn}\\), with \\(2n\\) absorbed by the logarithmic hypothesis. Induction gives matrices \\(C_t\\) such that\n\\[\n\\|C_t\\|,\\ \\|C_t^{-1}\\|\\le e^{Cbn},\\qquad\nB_t=C_{t+1}^{-1}A_tC_t,\n\\]\nwhere \\(B_t\\) is upper triangular with diagonal \\(e^{\\lambda_j}\\), no entries between the groups, and entries and inverse entries bounded by \\(e^{Cbn}\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\mathbb R^F\\) be the coordinate span indexed by \\(F\\), and let \\(\\mathbb R^{F^c}\\) be its coordinate complement. Set\n\\[\nE_t^f=C_t\\mathbb R^F,\\qquad E_t^s=C_t\\mathbb R^{F^c}.\n\\]\nThese spaces are invariant. Their projections are conjugates of coordinate projections, hence have the required bound. Each induced block is upper triangular in its inherited coordinate order, even if the two groups are interlaced. The triangular-product estimate within the blocks proves the two norm estimates.</p><p>The fast rates are exactly the \\(p\\) largest rates, so the general exterior bound gives the full degree-\\(p\\) upper estimate. Define\n\\[\nV_F(t)=\\sqrt{\\det\\!\\left((C_t|_{\\mathbb R^F})^*(C_t|_{\\mathbb R^F})\\right)}.\n\\]\nThe endpoint bounds give \\(e^{-Cbn}\\le V_F(t)\\le e^{Cbn}\\), while the normalized fast block has determinant \\(e^{\\Lambda l}\\). Thus\n\\[\nJ_p(A(t,s)|E_s^f)=e^{\\Lambda l}\\frac{V_F(t)}{V_F(s)}.\n\\]\nThis proves its upper and lower bounds. Applying the same argument to the inverse slow block proves the backward-volume bounds, for either sign of \\(\\Omega\\).</p><p>The normalized block decomposition splits each exterior power according to the number of fast and slow factors. In degree \\(p\\), every sector with a slow factor has diagonal rate sum at most \\(\\Lambda-2g\\): at least one fast rate has been replaced by a slow one. The triangular-product estimate bounds this sector by \\(e^{(\\Lambda-2g)l+Cbn}\\). If one original vector is slow, split the other vectors into fast and slow parts. There are only a fixed number of terms, each still with a slow factor. Projection norms and endpoint gauges are absorbed into \\(e^{Cbn}\\). This proves the mixed-factor estimate. Reversing time and exchanging the groups proves its backward counterpart.</p><p>For derivative matrices from Proposition 1, these conclusions apply in the moving orthonormal frames for all sufficiently large \\(k\\). A fixed uniformly regular atlas changes product estimates only by bounded endpoint conjugacies.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, “Common spaces in a bin.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the smooth setting, good witness frames, and parameters\n  \\[\n  n=2^k,\\quad b=b_k,\\quad B=b^{1/8},\\quad g=b^{1/2},\\quad\n  L=\\lceil b^{1/4}n\\rceil,\\quad h_0=e^{-Bn}\n  \\]\n  of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite family of coordinate charts with smaller relatively compact subcharts covering \\(M\\). Coordinate maps, inverse maps, and local expressions of \\(S,S^{-1}\\) have uniform bounds through order two on the relevant compact sets.</p><p class=\"li\">A spatial bin is contained in a smaller subchart, has coordinate diameter at most \\(Ch_0\\), and uses its specified chart to identify tangent spaces with \\(\\mathbb R^d\\). At the boundary use nested half-box charts.</p><p class=\"li\">For equal-dimensional subspaces \\(E,F\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\), their distance is \\(\\|\\Pi_E-\\Pi_F\\|\\), where \\(\\Pi_E,\\Pi_F\\) are orthogonal projections.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently large \\(k\\), every spatial bin \\(U\\) meeting \\(G_k\\) has\n\\[\n\\gamma_U\\in[g,(8d+1)g]\n\\]\nsuch that every good witness frame over \\(U\\) has all its rates at most \\(\\gamma_U-g\\) or at least \\(\\gamma_U+g\\), with the same number \\(p_U\\) in the second group. Apply Proposition 2 with this threshold.</p><p>There is \\(c&gt;0\\), independent of bins and witnesses, such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The spaces \\(E_0^s\\) from any two good witnesses over \\(U\\) are within \\(e^{-cgL}\\) in the chart of \\(U\\); the same holds for \\(E_0^f\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For good paths starting in \\(U\\) whose time-\\(n\\) endpoints lie in a common endpoint bin \\(V\\), their spaces \\(E_n^f\\) are within \\(e^{-cgL}\\) in the chart of \\(V\\). For paths whose time-\\((-n)\\) endpoints lie in a common bin, the analogous assertion holds for \\(E_{-n}^s\\).</p><p>Fix a representative endpoint space for each nonempty combination in the second assertion. Orthogonal projection onto it restricts to an isomorphism on every corresponding actual endpoint space, with inverse norm at most \\(2\\). If \\(p_U=0\\) or \\(d\\), the relevant zero or full spaces agree exactly.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a reference point in a starting bin. Uniform Lipschitz bounds for \\(S,S^{-1}\\) give\n\\[\nd_M(S^tx,S^ty)\\le CA_0^{|t|}h_0,\\qquad |t|\\le L,\n\\]\nfor another point in that bin and fixed \\(A_0&gt;1\\). Since \\(L=o(Bn)\\), this tends uniformly to zero. At each time on either short reference orbit, choose a chart whose smaller subchart contains the reference point; nearby orbits remain in its larger chart. Use the prescribed bin chart initially.</p><p>The one-step coordinate derivatives and inverses are uniformly bounded, and derivative differences are at most \\(CA_0^{|t|}h_0\\). The telescoping product identity gives\n\\[\n\\|P_x^\\pm-P_y^\\pm\\|\\le e^{-Bn+CL}=:\\zeta_k,\\qquad\n\\sigma_{\\min}(P_x^\\pm)\\ge e^{-CL}=:m_k,\n\\]\nwhere \\(P_x^+,P_x^-\\) represent \\(DS_x^L,DS_x^{-L}\\) in common initial and terminal charts. For example, summing the \\(L\\) product-difference terms gives \\(CLA_1^{2L}h_0\\). The inverse product bound gives the minimum stretch. The boundary case uses the same argument in nested half-box charts, or extensions of the finitely many local expressions.</p><p>Singular values are Lipschitz in operator norm by their min–max characterization. As \\(\\zeta_k/m_k\\to0\\),\n\\[\n|\\ln\\sigma_j(P_x^+)-\\ln\\sigma_j(P_y^+)|\n\\le2\\zeta_k/m_k.\n\\]\nFor a good witness \\(e\\), subtract the exterior-degree \\(j-1\\) estimate of Proposition 2 from its degree-\\(j\\) estimate:\n\\[\n\\left|L^{-1}\\ln\\sigma_j(P_{\\pi(e)}^+)-\\lambda_{(j)}(e)\\right|\n\\le Cbn/L.\n\\]\nFix a good witness \\(e_*\\) in \\(U\\). Every good witness in \\(U\\), including another witness over the same point, then satisfies\n\\[\n\\max_j|\\lambda_{(j)}(e)-\\lambda_{(j)}(e_*)|\n\\le Cbn/L+\\frac{2\\zeta_k}{m_kL}=o(g).\n\\]</p><p>Consider the \\(d+1\\) thresholds \\((1+8j)g\\), \\(0\\le j\\le d\\). A real number lies within distance \\(3g\\) of at most one candidate. The \\(d\\) representative rates therefore leave a candidate whose distance from all of them is greater than \\(3g\\). For large \\(k\\), every other sorted rate list differs by less than \\(g\\). This candidate separates every list with margin greater than \\(2g\\), hence with the required weaker margin \\(g\\); the fast dimension is constant.</p><p>Assume \\(0&lt;p_U&lt;d\\), and write \\(q=d-p_U\\). Use the single forward short map \\(P_*=P_{\\pi(e_*)}^+\\), and let \\(W\\) be its bottom \\(q\\) right-singular subspace. Its top \\(p_U\\) singular values are at least\n\\[\ne^{(\\gamma_U+g)L-Cbn}.\n\\]\nFor a unit vector \\(v\\) in the slow space of any good witness,\n\\[\n\\|P_*v\\|\\le e^{(\\gamma_U-g)L+Cbn}+\\zeta_k.\n\\]\nOrthogonality of the images of right-singular vectors implies\n\\[\n\\|\\Pi_{W^\\perp}v\\|\n\\le e^{-2gL+Cbn}+\\zeta_ke^{-(\\gamma_U+g)L+Cbn}\n\\le e^{-2gL+Cbn}+e^{-Bn+CL}\n\\le e^{-c_1gL}.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\|\\Pi_{W^\\perp}|E\\|\\le\\epsilon&lt;1\\) and \\(\\dim E=\\dim W\\), then \\(\\Pi_W|E\\) is an isomorphism and \\(E\\) is a graph over \\(W\\) of norm at most\n\\[\n\\epsilon/\\sqrt{1-\\epsilon^2}.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\epsilon\\le1/2\\), the corresponding orthogonal projectors differ by at most a fixed multiple of \\(\\epsilon\\). Thus all slow spaces are close to this same \\(W\\), and the triangle inequality compares them.</p><p>For fast spaces use the single backward short map \\(P_{\\pi(e_*)}^-\\) and its bottom \\(p_U\\) right-singular subspace. Its top \\(q\\) singular values are at least\n\\[\ne^{-(\\gamma_U-g)L-Cbn},\n\\]\nwhereas a unit fast vector has backward stretch at most \\(e^{-(\\gamma_U+g)L+Cbn}\\). The same calculation gives\n\\[\ne^{-2gL+Cbn}+\\zeta_ke^{(\\gamma_U-g)L+Cbn}\n\\le e^{-2gL+Cbn}+e^{-Bn+CL}.\n\\]\nThis proves the starting-space comparisons. Their error is \\(o(e^{-C_0bn})\\) for every fixed \\(C_0\\), since \\(bn=o(gL)\\).</p><p>For endpoints at time \\(n\\) in a common bin \\(V\\), compare backward short products of length \\(L\\) from the endpoints. Initial closeness for this comparison comes from \\(V\\), not from propagation for \\(n\\) steps. The intervals \\([n-L,n]\\) lie inside the good interval; Proposition 2 applies on each with the same full-segment rates, threshold, and group dimension. The backward argument compares \\(E_n^f\\). Forward short products on \\([-n,-n+L]\\) compare \\(E_{-n}^s\\).</p><p>Finally, if \\(\\|\\Pi_E-\\Pi_F\\|\\le\\epsilon\\), then\n\\[\n\\|\\Pi_Fv\\|\\ge\\sqrt{1-\\epsilon^2}\\|v\\|,\\qquad v\\in E.\n\\]\nEqual dimensions give surjectivity, and the inverse norm is at most \\((1-\\epsilon^2)^{-1/2}\\le2\\). Empty-group cases are immediate.</p><p>Choosing orthonormal bases within a representative slow and fast space in each starting bin gives a fixed product-basis matrix with bounded forward norm and inverse norm at most \\(e^{Cbn}\\), by Proposition 2. These choices, and the endpoint projections, are finite at each scale.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 3.3, “Exterior-product perturbation.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix \\(d\\ge1\\), \\(K\\ge1\\), \\(0\\le p\\le d\\), and \\(m\\ge1\\). Let \\(A_0,\\ldots,A_{m-1}\\) and \\(\\widehat A_0,\\ldots,\\widehat A_{m-1}\\) be real \\(d\\times d\\) matrices satisfying\n\\[\n\\|A_t\\|\\le K,\\qquad \\|\\widehat A_t-A_t\\|\\le\\epsilon\\le1.\n\\]\nUse the product notation of Proposition 2 for nonnegative indices. Suppose \\(a\\ge0\\), \\(\\sigma\\in\\mathbb R\\), and\n\\[\n\\|\\bigwedge^pA(t,s)\\|\\le e^{\\sigma(t-s)+a},\n\\qquad 0\\le s\\le t\\le m.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(C=C(d,K)\\) such that\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\bigwedge^p\\widehat A(m,0)-\\bigwedge^pA(m,0)\\right\\|\n\\le e^{\\sigma m+a}\\left[(1+C\\epsilon e^{a+|\\sigma|})^m-1\\right].\n\\]\nFor \\(p=0\\), the left side is zero.</p><p>In particular, use the scales\n\\[\nn=2^k,\\quad b=b_k,\\quad B=b^{1/8}\n\\]\nof Proposition 1, and suppose\n\\[\nm\\le2n,\\qquad a\\le C_1bn,\\qquad |\\sigma|\\le C_2,\\qquad\n\\epsilon\\le C_3e^{-3Bn/2},\n\\]\nwith fixed constants. For every fixed \\(C_0&gt;0\\), the left side is\n\\[\no(e^{\\sigma m-C_0bn})\n\\]\nuniformly over matrices satisfying these hypotheses.</p>", "proof": "<p>Exterior power is multiplicative. Multilinearity in its \\(p\\) columns and the one-step bound \\(K+1\\) give\n\\[\nE_t:=\\bigwedge^p\\widehat A_t-\\bigwedge^pA_t,\\qquad\n\\|E_t\\|\\le C\\epsilon.\n\\]\nExpand the product using either the unperturbed exterior matrix or \\(E_t\\) at each time. A term with \\(q\\ge1\\) insertions has \\(q+1\\) unperturbed intervals of total length \\(m-q\\). Allowing empty intervals, its norm is at most\n\\[\ne^{\\sigma(m-q)+(q+1)a}(C\\epsilon)^q\n=e^{\\sigma m+a}(C\\epsilon e^{a-\\sigma})^q.\n\\]\nThere are \\(\\binom mq\\) choices of insertion positions. Sum over \\(q=1,\\ldots,m\\) and use \\(-\\sigma\\le|\\sigma|\\). This proves the estimate; negative \\(\\sigma\\) causes only the displayed factor for each removed step.</p><p>For the final assertion, put \\(u=C\\epsilon e^{a+|\\sigma|}\\) and use\n\\[\n(1+u)^m-1\\le mu e^{mu}.\n\\]\nAfter division by \\(e^{\\sigma m-C_0bn}\\), the bound is at most\n\\[\nCm\\epsilon e^{(2C_1+C_0)bn+C_2}\n\\exp(Cm\\epsilon e^{C_1bn+C_2}).\n\\]\nThe logarithm of its first factor is at most\n\\[\n-3Bn/2+O(bn)+\\ln(2n)\\longrightarrow-\\infty.\n\\]\nThe quantity in its second exponential tends to zero for the same reason. All constants are fixed. For degree zero, both products are the identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Theorem 4.1, “Smooth switching”; used as an intermediate result for Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(M\\) be a compact smooth manifold of positive finite dimension, let \\(\\nu\\) be a smooth positive probability volume in the density sense of Theorem 1, and let \\(S:M\\to M\\) be a smooth diffeomorphism preserving \\(\\nu\\). Fix a Riemannian distance \\(d_M\\) and \\(\\eta&gt;0\\). Smooth boundary is allowed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are positive numbers \\(B_k\\) with\n\\[\n\\sum_kB_k^{16}&lt;\\infty\n\\]\nand a constant \\(C\\) such that, for all sufficiently large \\(k\\), with \\(n=2^k\\), there is a finite measurable partition \\(\\mathcal D_k\\) with\n\\[\n\\#\\mathcal D_k\\le\\exp(CB_kn).\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\n\\rho_k=\n\\sum_{\\substack{Q\\in\\mathcal D_k\\\\\\nu(Q)&gt;0}}\n\\frac{(\\nu|_Q)\\otimes(\\nu|_Q)}{\\nu(Q)}.\n\\]\nThus one samples a cell with its \\(\\nu\\)-weight and then two independent points from its normalized volume.</p><p>There are a measurable set \\(G_k\\subset M\\times M\\), with \\(\\rho_k(G_k)\\ge1-\\eta\\), and a measurable map \\(z_k:G_k\\to M\\) such that\n\\[\n(z_k)_*(\\rho_k|_{G_k})\\le2\\nu\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sup_{(x,y)\\in G_k}\n\\left[\n\\max_{0\\le t\\le n}d_M(S^tz_k(x,y),S^tx)\n+\n\\max_{-n\\le t\\le0}d_M(S^tz_k(x,y),S^ty)\n\\right]\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThe output measure is not normalized after restriction to \\(G_k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The sequence from Proposition 1 gives\n\\[\n\\sum_kB_k^{16}=\\sum_kb_k^2&lt;\\infty;\n\\]\nfinitely many unused terms may be chosen positively. Lemma 5.1 proves the partition bound and retained-mass estimate. Lemma 5.2 gives uniform shadowing, since \\(e^{Cgn}r\\to0\\).</p><p>On the square of a positive-volume cell \\(Q'\\), the law \\(\\rho_k\\) has density \\(1/\\nu(Q')\\) with respect to \\(\\nu\\otimes\\nu\\). The map \\(\\Phi\\) sends retained pairs in that square into the same square. By Lemma 5.3 its inverse volume Jacobian is at most \\(2\\) for large \\(k\\). Apply change of variables on the countably many pieces of Lemma 5.4. Their images are disjoint by global injectivity. Therefore\n\\[\n\\Phi_*(\\rho_k|_{G_k})\\le2\\rho_k.\n\\]\nThe factor \\(1/\\nu(Q')\\) is unchanged. Restrictions to endpoint-label pieces or to \\(G_k\\) are never normalized, and zero-volume cells contribute nothing. Taking first marginals gives\n\\[\n(z_k)_*(\\rho_k|_{G_k})\\le2\\nu,\n\\]\nbecause the first marginal of \\(\\rho_k\\) is \\(\\nu\\). The construction includes smooth boundary and does not require orientation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Section 4.1, “Grids, retained pairs, and fixed endpoint projections.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the setting of Proposition 5 and assume \\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;1\\). Apply Proposition 1 with parameter \\(\\eta/2\\), denote the resulting good base set by \\(A_k\\), and put\n\\[\nn=2^k,\\quad b=b_k,\\quad B=b^{1/8},\\quad h_0=e^{-Bn},\\quad\nr=e^{-2Bn},\n\\]\n\\[\nL=\\lceil b^{1/4}n\\rceil,\\quad g=b^{1/2},\\quad\n\\delta=e^{-3Bn/2}.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(\\nu(M\\setminus A_k)&lt;\\eta/100\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently large \\(k\\), one can make the following finite choices.</p><p>Choose disjoint chart regions by assigning each point to the first smaller coordinate box containing it. In each region, retain grid cubes of side \\(h_0\\) whose closures lie in the region; call these coarse bins. For a coarse starting bin \\(Q\\) meeting \\(A_k\\), use Proposition 3 to fix a threshold \\(\\gamma_Q\\in[g,Cg]\\), a fast dimension \\(p_Q\\), and reference slow and fast spaces. Let\n\\[\nE_Q:\\mathbb R^{d-p_Q}\\times\\mathbb R^{p_Q}\\to\\mathbb R^d\n\\]\nhave orthonormal columns within the two spaces, slow first. Tile the chart by\n\\[\nE_Q([0,r)^d+rj),\\qquad j\\in\\mathbb Z^d,\n\\]\nretaining only cells whose closures lie in \\(Q\\). Together with one additional cell for omitted points, these form \\(\\mathcal D_k\\), and\n\\[\n\\#\\mathcal D_k\\le e^{CBn}.\n\\]</p><p>There are a fixed \\(\\tau&gt;0\\) and a Borel set \\(R_k\\subset A_k\\) consisting of points:</p><p class=\"li\">lying in full product cells with every product coordinate at least \\(\\tau r\\) from its cell faces;</p><p class=\"li\">whose endpoints \\(S^{\\pm n}u\\) lie in coarse bins at coordinate distance at least \\(\\tau h_0\\) from their faces;</p><p class=\"li\">in the boundary case, with every integer iterate in \\([-n,n]\\) at distance greater than \\(c_0h_0\\) from \\(\\partial M\\);</p><p>such that\n\\[\n\\nu(M\\setminus R_k)&lt;\\eta/4.\n\\]\nFor\n\\[\nG_k=(R_k\\times R_k)\\cap\n\\bigcup_{Q'\\text{ a full product cell}}(Q'\\times Q'),\n\\]\none has \\(\\rho_k(G_k)\\ge1-\\eta/2\\).</p><p>For each relevant pair of starting and endpoint bins, choose fixed endpoint projections\n\\[\nP^+_{Q,U}:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^{p_Q},\\qquad\nP^-_{Q,V}:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^{d-p_Q},\n\\]\nby orthogonal projection onto representative fast time-\\(n\\) and slow time-\\((-n)\\) spaces, followed by orthonormal identifications. They restrict to isomorphisms on the corresponding actual endpoint spaces of all good paths, with inverse norm at most \\(2\\).</p><p>For \\((x,y)\\in G_k\\), write \\(Q\\) for its starting coarse bin and \\(U_x,V_x,U_y,V_y\\) for the bins of \\(S^nx,S^{-n}x,S^ny,S^{-n}y\\). With \\(\\chi_U\\) the fixed chart for bin \\(U\\), define\n\\[\nF_x(u)=P^+_{Q,U_x}\\chi_{U_x}(S^nu),\\qquad\nP_x(u)=P^-_{Q,V_x}\\chi_{V_x}(S^{-n}u),\n\\]\n\\[\nF_y(u)=P^+_{Q,U_y}\\chi_{U_y}(S^nu),\\qquad\nP_y(u)=P^-_{Q,V_y}\\chi_{V_y}(S^{-n}u).\n\\]\nHere the subscripts indicate fixed bin labels. The complementary switching equations are\n\\[\nF_x(z)=F_x(x),\\qquad P_y(z)=P_y(y),\n\\]\n\\[\nF_y(w)=F_y(y),\\qquad P_x(w)=P_x(x).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The boundaries of the finitely many chart regions lie in finitely many smooth coordinate faces. Coordinate maps, inverses, and chart densities have uniform bounds on slightly larger compact patches, and the densities have a uniform positive lower bound. The coarse-bin discarded part lies in an \\(O(h_0)\\)-neighborhood of those faces, so its volume is \\(O(h_0)\\).</p><p>Propositions 2 and 3 give\n\\[\n\\|E_Q^{-1}\\|\\le e^{Cbn},\\qquad \\|E_Q\\|\\le\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nIndeed, for a slow-fast pair \\((a,b')\\),\n\\[\n|E_Q(a,b')|\\le|a|+|b'|.\n\\]\nEvery product cell has physical diameter at most \\(C_dr\\) and volume at least \\(e^{-Cbn}r^d\\), up to a fixed factor. Thus the total number of full cells is at most\n\\[\nCe^{Cbn}r^{-d}\\le e^{CBn}.\n\\]\nEvery point farther than \\(C_dr\\) from a coarse-bin wall belongs to a full product cell. The total partial-cell loss is therefore \\(O(r/h_0)=o(1)\\). Adding one omitted-points cell does not change the partition bound.</p><p>In the product coordinates of a cell, the ideal exchange\n\\[\nx=(x_s,x_f),\\quad y=(y_s,y_f),\\qquad\nz_0=(y_s,x_f),\\quad w_0=(x_s,y_f)\n\\]\nstays in that cell.</p><p>Product-coordinate strips of width \\(\\tau r\\) remove at most \\(2d\\tau\\) of each cell’s Euclidean volume. The determinant of \\(E_Q\\) cancels in this fraction. The ratio between the largest and smallest smooth density on a physical cell is \\(1+O(r)\\), so their total smooth volume is at most \\(2d\\tau+o(1)\\). Coarse-bin strips of width \\(\\tau h_0\\) have the same type of bound. These estimates concern the original measure, without conditioning on goodness.</p><p>If \\(M\\) has boundary, a smooth boundary collar has volume \\(O(h_0)\\). Invariance bounds the loss from all integer times in \\([-n,n]\\) by \\(O(nh_0)=o(1)\\), since \\(Bn\\ge n^{79/80}\\). Local extensions across the boundary provide uniform derivative bounds; the orbit tubes constructed later remain in \\(M\\).</p><p>The losses and invariance give\n\\[\n\\nu(M\\setminus R_k)\\le\\eta/100+C_d\\tau+o(1).\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\tau\\) with \\(C_d\\tau&lt;\\eta/100\\), then increase \\(k\\) until this is less than \\(\\eta/4\\). Both marginals of \\(\\rho_k\\) are exactly \\(\\nu\\). Since \\(R_k\\) meets only full product cells,\n\\[\n\\rho_k(G_k)=\\rho_k(R_k\\times R_k)\\ge1-\\eta/2.\n\\]\nNo uniform distribution of good points within individual cells is required.</p><p>The fixed endpoint projections and their inverse bounds are precisely the endpoint conclusion of Proposition 3. All choices are finite at each scale; no measurable frame selection is involved.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the grids, retained set, endpoint maps, and scale parameters of Lemma 5.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every sufficiently large \\(k\\) and every \\((x,y)\\in G_k\\), the equations\n\\[\nF_x(z)=F_x(x),\\qquad P_y(z)=P_y(y)\n\\]\nhave a solution satisfying\n\\[\n\\max_{0\\le t\\le n}d_M(S^tz,S^tx)\n+\n\\max_{-n\\le t\\le0}d_M(S^tz,S^ty)\n\\le e^{Cgn}r.\n\\]\nIn fixed regular coordinates along the two reference halves, the solution is unique among displacement arrays of sup norm at most \\(\\delta\\).</p><p>There is a complementary solution \\(w\\), with \\(x,y\\) exchanged. In the common cell’s product coordinates,\n\\[\nz=(y_s,x_f)+o(r),\\qquad w=(x_s,y_f)+o(r),\n\\]\nuniformly over retained pairs. Both outputs remain in the original product cell, and their relevant endpoints remain in the four original endpoint bins.</p>", "proof": "<p>At every reference location choose a regular patch containing a fixed-radius neighborhood. At time zero use \\(\\chi_Q\\) for both strings, and at the terminal times use the specified endpoint charts. After shrinking the fixed radius, all one-step coordinate maps, their first two derivatives, and the corresponding inverse derivatives have uniform bounds. These bounds come from a finite atlas and compactness, not from a uniform bound on the second derivative of \\(S^n\\). Internal patches may be chosen separately at each time.</p><p>Use displacement variables \\(v_t^+\\) about \\(S^tx\\), \\(0\\le t\\le n\\), and \\(v_t^-\\) about \\(S^ty\\), \\(-n\\le t\\le0\\). Let \\(A_t^\\pm\\) be the derivative steps in these charts. The linear system is\n\\[\nv_{t+1}^\\pm-A_t^\\pm v_t^\\pm=f_t^\\pm,\n\\]\n\\[\nv_0^+-v_0^-=m,\\qquad\nP^+_{Q,U_x}v_n^+=h_+,\\qquad\nP^-_{Q,V_y}v_{-n}^-=h_-.\n\\]\nFor the actual problem,\n\\[\nm=\\chi_Q(y)-\\chi_Q(x),\\qquad |m|=O(r),\n\\]\nand terminal data are zero.</p><p>Put \\(\\gamma=\\gamma_Q\\) and \\(\\widetilde v_t=e^{-\\gamma t}v_t\\), weighting step data at their output times \\(t+1\\). By Proposition 2, weighted slow-forward and fast-backward propagators have norm at most\n\\[\ne^{Cbn}e^{-g|t-s|}.\n\\]\nTheir projections cost \\(e^{Cbn}\\), and geometric sums cost at most \\(C/g\\le e^{Cbn}\\).</p><p>On the future half prescribe \\(a\\in E_0^s(x)\\), propagate its slow part forward, and add the projected step data. If \\(s_n\\) is its slow value at time \\(n\\), the fast value there is uniquely\n\\[\nc_n=\n(P^+_{Q,U_x}|_{E_n^f(x)})^{-1}\n(\\widetilde h_+-P^+_{Q,U_x}s_n).\n\\]\nPropagate this value and the fast step data backward. All forced terms are bounded by \\(e^{Cbn}\\) times the weighted-data sup norm. The dependence of the fast value at zero on \\(a\\) is\n\\[\nK_+=-\\widetilde A_x(0,n)|_{E_n^f(x)}\n(P^+_{Q,U_x}|_{E_n^f(x)})^{-1}\nP^+_{Q,U_x}\\widetilde A_x(n,0)|_{E_0^s(x)},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\widetilde A(t,s)=e^{-\\gamma(t-s)}A(t,s)\\), and\n\\[\n\\|K_+\\|\\le e^{-2gn+Cbn}.\n\\]\nBoth decaying factors are present; the endpoint projection need not annihilate the slow space. Hence\n\\[\n\\widetilde v_0^+=a+K_+a+q_+.\n\\]</p><p>On the past half prescribe \\(b'\\in E_0^f(y)\\), propagate fast backward, use the slow endpoint projection to determine the slow value at \\(-n\\), then propagate slow forward. This gives\n\\[\n\\widetilde v_0^-=b'+K_-b'+q_-,\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n\\|K_-\\|\\le e^{-2gn+Cbn},\\qquad\n\\|q_-\\|\\le e^{Cbn}\\|\\text{weighted data}\\|_\\infty.\n\\]</p><p>The map\n\\[\n(a,b')\\longmapsto a-b',\n\\qquad E_0^s(x)\\oplus E_0^f(y)\\to\\mathbb R^d,\n\\]\nhas inverse norm at most \\(e^{Cbn}\\). The reference spaces are complementary with this bound, and their perturbations are \\(e^{-cgL}\\), which remain small after multiplication by the reference inverse. The additional \\(K_+,K_-\\) terms are smaller still. A Neumann series solves the matching equation with cost \\(e^{Cbn}\\). Thus the weighted full system has inverse cost \\(e^{Cbn}\\). Removing weights costs at most \\(e^{2\\gamma(n+1)}\\), giving\n\\[\n\\|\\mathcal L^{-1}\\|_{\\infty\\to\\infty}\\le Q_k,\\qquad Q_k=e^{Cgn}.\n\\]\nIf a group is empty, omit its variables, propagation formula, and terminal datum. The same bounds apply.</p><p>Taylor expansion of each one-step map gives a residual \\(R(v)\\) satisfying, on the sup-norm \\(\\delta\\)-ball,\n\\[\n\\|R(v)\\|_\\infty\\le C\\|v\\|_\\infty^2,\\qquad\n\\|R(v)-R(u)\\|_\\infty\\le C\\delta\\|v-u\\|_\\infty.\n\\]\nEach residual coordinate depends on just one step, so there is no factor \\(n\\). Matching and terminal equations are affine. The nonlinear problem is\n\\[\nv=\\mathcal L^{-1}(a_0+R(v)),\\qquad \\|a_0\\|_\\infty\\le Cr.\n\\]\nThe scale comparisons imply\n\\[\nCQ_k\\delta\\to0,\\qquad Q_kr/\\delta\\to0.\n\\]\nFor large \\(k\\), this map sends the \\(\\delta\\)-ball into its \\(\\delta/2\\)-ball and has Lipschitz constant at most \\(1/4\\). It has a unique fixed point there, with\n\\[\n\\|v\\|_\\infty\\le2CQ_kr.\n\\]\nThe exact step equations and matching equation produce one genuine orbit, whose time-zero point is \\(z\\). Uniform chart bounds prove the shadowing estimate. In the boundary case, its distance from each reference orbit is \\(o(h_0)\\), so it stays inside \\(M\\).</p><p>For the linear solution with zero step and terminal data, the initial displacement spaces are the graphs just constructed. In reference product coordinates they differ from the slow and fast coordinate spaces by at most\n\\[\ne^{Cbn}(e^{-cgL}+e^{-2gn+Cbn}).\n\\]\nSolving the matching equation therefore differs from ideal exchange by at most\n\\[\ne^{Cbn}(e^{-cgL}+e^{-2gn+Cbn})r=o(r).\n\\]\nAll basis-conversion and matching-inverse factors are included in \\(C\\). The nonlinear solution differs from the linear one by at most\n\\[\nCQ_k(2CQ_kr)^2=e^{Cgn}r^2,\n\\]\nand still by \\(e^{Cgn}r^2=o(r)\\) in product coordinates. This proves the time-zero formulas. The argument for \\(w\\) exchanges the inputs.</p><p>For large \\(k\\), product-coordinate errors are less than \\(\\tau r/4\\), so outputs have margins at least \\(3\\tau r/4\\). Endpoint errors are less than \\(\\tau h_0/4\\), so relevant endpoints have margins at least \\(3\\tau h_0/4\\).</p><p>The solution is independent of internal charts or frame witnesses: any two constructions satisfy the same endpoint equations and have radius \\(e^{Cgn}r=o(\\delta)\\). Both lie in either construction’s uniqueness ball and therefore coincide.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>For the fixed labels of a retained pair from Lemma 5.1, define\n\\[\nH_{ab}(u)=(F_a(u),P_b(u)),\\qquad a,b\\in\\{x,y\\}.\n\\]\nLet \\(z,w\\) be the complementary solutions of Lemma 5.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The derivatives\n\\[\nDH_{xx}(x),\\quad DH_{yy}(y),\\quad DH_{xy}(z),\\quad DH_{yx}(w)\n\\]\nare nonsingular. Locally, the switching equations define a smooth pair map\n\\[\n\\Phi(x,y)=(z,w)\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\nJ_{\\nu\\otimes\\nu}\\Phi(x,y)=1+o(1)\n\\]\nuniformly on retained pairs. This includes empty slow or fast groups.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(p=p_Q\\). For the future map at \\(x\\), first use orthonormal bases within its actual fast and slow spaces. The fast \\(p\\)-minor has absolute value at least\n\\[\ne^{\\Lambda_xn-Cbn},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\Lambda_x\\) is the sum of its fast rates. This follows from Proposition 2’s fast-volume lower bound and the endpoint projection’s invertible restriction. Every other maximal minor contains a slow starting vector and, when both groups occur, is at most\n\\[\ne^{(\\Lambda_x-2g)n+Cbn}.\n\\]</p><p>Replacing actual bases by the common reference basis changes the spaces by \\(e^{-cgL}\\). After orthonormal basis changes within each space, multilinearity and the full exterior bound show that the leading minor stays nonzero and every other maximal minor is relatively negligible. Oblique-basis inverse factors cost \\(e^{Cbn}\\). Denote the resulting leading absolute minor by \\(A_x\\). More explicitly, every nonleading maximal future minor \\(\\Delta\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\frac{|\\Delta|}{A_x}\n\\le C(e^{-2gn+Cbn}+e^{-cgL+Cbn})=o(1).\n\\]\nIf \\(p=0\\) or \\(d\\), no nonleading future minors occur. Define \\(A_y\\) similarly.</p><p>For the past maps the leading columns are slow. Put\n\\[\n\\Omega_x=-\\sum_{\\text{slow at }x}\\lambda_j(x),\n\\]\nand similarly for \\(y\\). Denote leading absolute past minors by \\(B_x,B_y\\). Their lower bounds are\n\\[\ne^{\\Omega_xn-Cbn},\\qquad e^{\\Omega_yn-Cbn},\n\\]\nand all other maximal minors are relatively negligible. No sign condition on \\(\\Lambda_x\\) or \\(\\Omega_x\\) is used.</p><p>Transfer future minors from \\(x\\) to \\(z\\) in the common charts along the future of \\(x\\). Lemma 5.2 bounds the orbit displacement by \\(o(\\delta)\\), so one-step derivative differences are at most \\(C\\delta\\). On every unperturbed subinterval the degree-\\(p\\) exterior norm is at most\n\\[\ne^{\\Lambda_x\\ell+Cbn}.\n\\]\nProposition 4 therefore gives, for every fixed \\(C_0\\),\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\bigwedge^pD(S^n)_z-\\bigwedge^pD(S^n)_x\\right\\|\n=o(e^{\\Lambda_xn-C_0bn}).\n\\]\nAn expansion term with \\(q\\ge1\\) altered factors is bounded by\n\\[\ne^{\\Lambda_x(n-q)+(q+1)C_1bn}(C\\delta)^q.\n\\]\nThe binomial sum divided by \\(e^{\\Lambda_xn-C_0bn}\\) tends to zero because \\(n\\delta e^{Cbn}\\to0\\). The bounded factor \\(e^{q|\\Lambda_x|}\\) covers either sign of the rate. Backward products use \\(\\Omega_y\\) and the same argument.</p><p>Fixed endpoint projections and the common starting basis preserve these relative estimates, increasing \\(C_0\\) if necessary. At \\(z\\), leading future and past minors are\n\\[\nA_x(1+o(1)),\\qquad B_y(1+o(1)),\n\\]\nwith nonleading minors relatively negligible. At \\(w\\), they are \\(A_y,B_x\\).</p><p>Expand each full determinant along its \\(p\\) future rows. The leading product uses fast future columns and slow past columns; every other term contains a relatively negligible minor. In the common starting basis,\n\\[\n|\\det DH_{xx}(x)|=A_xB_x(1+o(1)),\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\det DH_{yy}(y)|=A_yB_y(1+o(1)),\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\det DH_{xy}(z)|=A_xB_y(1+o(1)),\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\det DH_{yx}(w)|=A_yB_x(1+o(1)).\n\\]\nAll leading factors are positive, proving local invertibility. For \\(p=0\\), put \\(A_x=A_y=1\\); for \\(p=d\\), put \\(B_x=B_y=1\\).</p><p>The endpoint equations say that\n\\[\n(H_{xy}(z),H_{yx}(w))\n\\]\nis a fixed permutation of\n\\[\n(H_{xx}(x),H_{yy}(y)).\n\\]\nDifferentiation gives the exact identity\n\\[\n|\\det D\\Phi(x,y)|\n=\\frac{|\\det DH_{xx}(x)|\\,|\\det DH_{yy}(y)|}\n{|\\det DH_{xy}(z)|\\,|\\det DH_{yx}(w)|}.\n\\]\nThe two common-basis determinant factors in the numerator cancel the two in the denominator. The factors \\(A_x,B_x,A_y,B_y\\) cancel individually, without comparing rates of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). The coordinate Jacobian is therefore \\(1+o(1)\\).</p><p>All four starting points lie in the same cell of diameter \\(O(r)\\). The ratio of output smooth densities to input smooth densities is also \\(1+o(1)\\), proving the volume-Jacobian assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "Lemma 4.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the retained set \\(G_k\\), complementary joins \\(z,w\\), and pair map\n\\[\n\\Phi:G_k\\to M\\times M,\\qquad \\Phi(x,y)=(z,w),\n\\]\nof Lemmas 5.1–5.3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map \\(\\Phi\\) is measurable and injective. Its restrictions to countably many measurable pieces agree with smooth local diffeomorphisms having the Jacobian of Lemma 5.3.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a retained pair. Its cell and four endpoint labels are locally constant among nearby retained inputs because of positive wall margins. Lemma 5.3 gives a smooth local implicit pair branch near its outputs.</p><p>This branch agrees with the constructed switch on nearby retained inputs. For fixed \\(k\\), continuity of the finitely many maps \\(S^t\\), \\(|t|\\le n\\), makes their reference halves arbitrarily close as inputs approach. Constructed solutions have radius \\(e^{Cgn}r=o(\\delta)\\). Both the old local implicit branch and the nearby construction therefore lie in the old \\(\\delta\\)-tube and satisfy the same endpoint equations with nearby right-hand sides.</p><p>The contraction uniqueness argument still applies to changed data: two solutions \\(u,v\\) in the old ball satisfy\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(v-u)=R(v)-R(u),\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n\\|v-u\\|_\\infty\\le CQ_k\\delta\\|v-u\\|_\\infty\n\\]\nforces equality. Both arrays use the old reference halves, so their matching datum is the same old coordinate difference; only endpoint values change. No regularity of good-frame choices is required.</p><p>The retained set is Borel, and \\(M\\times M\\) is second countable. A countable collection of these relative neighborhoods covers \\(G_k\\). Subtracting earlier members gives countably many Borel pieces on each of which \\(\\Phi\\) is a smooth local diffeomorphism restricted to the piece. This proves measurability and supplies change-of-variables pieces.</p><p>For injectivity, suppose\n\\[\n\\Phi(x,y)=\\Phi(x',y')=(z,w).\n\\]\nOutputs remain in their original cell, and product-cell interiors are disjoint. Thus both input pairs have the same product cell, starting bin, threshold, and dimensions. Recover the four endpoint labels from the ordered outputs:\n\\[\n(U_x,V_x,U_y,V_y)\n=\n(\\operatorname{bin}(S^nz),\\operatorname{bin}(S^{-n}w),\n\\operatorname{bin}(S^nw),\\operatorname{bin}(S^{-n}z)).\n\\]\nPositive endpoint margins make these labels unambiguous. Hence all four projections and coordinate maps are identical for the competing inputs.</p><p>On the future half, \\(x,x'\\) are within \\(e^{Cgn}r\\) of \\(z\\); on the past half they are within that distance of \\(w\\). Their full orbits on \\([-n,n]\\) are therefore within \\(2e^{Cgn}r=o(\\delta)\\). The recovered equations give\n\\[\nF_x(x')=F_x(z)=F_x(x),\\qquad\nP_x(x')=P_x(w)=P_x(x).\n\\]\nApply the boundary uniqueness argument of Lemma 5.2 with both reference halves equal to the true good orbit of \\(x\\), using its endpoint projections and zero matching and terminal data. The orbits of \\(x,x'\\) are two solutions in the same \\(\\delta\\)-ball, hence \\(x'=x\\). Likewise \\(y'=y\\). Neither output is required to be good.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 5.1, “Finite word families.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>For \\(i\\ge1\\), set\n\\[\n\\epsilon_i=2^{-i-2},\\qquad L_i=2^{2s_i}-1,\\qquad\n\\ell_i=\\log L_i,\\qquad K_i=\\lfloor\\sqrt{\\ell_i}\\rfloor,\n\\]\n\\[\nN_i=2^{K_i},\\qquad H_i=2^{\\lfloor K_i/2\\rfloor},\\qquad\nD_i^*=2^{\\lfloor K_i/8\\rfloor},\n\\]\n\\[\nm_1=1,\\qquad m_{i+1}=m_iN_i.\n\\]\nChoose \\(s_1\\) so that \\(K_1\\ge256\\). Recursively, let \\(2s_{i+1}\\) be the greatest even integer not exceeding\n\\[\nX_i=\\frac{\\epsilon_i}{100}N_i\\ell_i.\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_i=\\mathbb F_2^{2s_i}\\setminus\\{0\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal A=\\mathcal A_1,\n\\]\nand equip \\(\\mathbb F_2^{2s_i}\\) with the nondegenerate alternating form\n\\[\n[u,v]_i=\\sum_{a=1}^{s_i}(u_av_{s_i+a}+u_{s_i+a}v_a)\\in\\mathbb F_2.\n\\]\nEach family \\(\\mathcal W_i\\subset\\mathcal A^{m_i}\\), once constructed, is identified by a fixed bijection with \\(\\mathcal A_i\\). For a concatenation \\(w\\) of \\(N_i\\) words in \\(\\mathcal W_i\\), write\n\\[\nc_i(w)=(u_0,\\ldots,u_{N_i-1})\\in\\mathcal W_i^{N_i}.\n\\]\nPositions start at zero. The Hamming distance \\(d_H\\) counts unequal entries in the alphabet being used. Fix \\(\\vartheta=10^{-4}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The recursion is well defined at every level, and there are families\n\\[\n\\mathcal W_i\\subset\\mathcal A^{m_i},\\qquad \\#\\mathcal W_i=L_i,\\qquad\n\\mathcal W_1=\\mathcal A,\n\\]\nsuch that every member of \\(\\mathcal W_{i+1}\\) concatenates \\(N_i\\) members of \\(\\mathcal W_i\\), and the following hold.</p><p>\\(\\mathrm{(W1)}\\) Relations within groups. For \\(w\\in\\mathcal W_{i+1}\\), \\(c_i(w)=(u_r)_r\\), every \\(0\\le b&lt;N_i/H_i\\), every \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor K_i/8\\rfloor\\), and every \\(0\\le r&lt;r+2^k&lt;H_i\\),\n\\[\n[u_{bH_i+r},u_{bH_i+r+2^k}]_i=0.\n\\]</p><p>\\(\\mathrm{(W2)}\\) Distances. Distinct \\(v,w\\in\\mathcal W_{i+1}\\) satisfy\n\\[\nd_H(c_i(v),c_i(w))\\ge(1-\\epsilon_i)N_i.\n\\]\nFor all \\(v,w,z\\in\\mathcal W_{i+1}\\), allowing repetitions, and \\(1\\le t&lt;N_i\\),\n\\[\nd_H((c_i(v)c_i(w))[t,t+N_i),c_i(z))\n\\ge(1-\\epsilon_i)N_i.\n\\]</p><p>\\(\\mathrm{(W3)}\\) Short-window laws. If \\(U\\) is uniform on \\(\\mathcal W_{i+1}\\), then for every \\(0\\le t\\le N_i-D_i^*\\) there is a probability \\(Q_{i,t}\\) on \\(\\mathcal W_i^{D_i^*}\\) such that\n\\[\n\\operatorname{TV}\\bigl(\\mathcal L(c_i(U)[t,t+D_i^*)),Q_{i,t}\\bigr)\\le\\vartheta.\n\\]\nFor \\(1\\le d'\\le D_i^*\\), distinct \\(0\\le r_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;r_{d'}&lt;D_i^*\\), and prescribed \\(v_1,\\ldots,v_{d'}\\in\\mathcal W_i\\),\n\\[\nQ_{i,t}\\{u:u_{r_j}=v_j,\\ 1\\le j\\le d'\\}\n\\le2\\left(\\frac{2^{2K_i}}{L_i}\\right)^{d'}.\n\\]</p><p>\\(\\mathrm{(W4)}\\) Per-word approximation. If \\(i\\ge2\\), \\(w\\in\\mathcal W_{i+1}\\) is fixed, \\(c_i(w)=(u_r)_r\\), \\(J\\) is uniform on \\(\\{0,\\ldots,N_i-1\\}\\), and \\(U\\) is uniform on \\(\\mathcal W_i\\), then for every \\(0\\le t\\le N_{i-1}-D_{i-1}^*\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{TV}\\!\\left(\n\\mathcal L(c_{i-1}(u_J)[t,t+D_{i-1}^*)),\n\\mathcal L(c_{i-1}(U)[t,t+D_{i-1}^*))\n\\right)\\le\\vartheta.\n\\]\nThe first law averages constituents of the single fixed word \\(w\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Whenever \\(X_i\\ge6\\), rounding gives\n\\[\nX_i-3&lt;\\ell_{i+1}\\le X_i,\\qquad\n\\ell_{i+1}\\ge X_i/2=\\frac{\\epsilon_i}{200}2^{K_i}\\ell_i.\n\\]\nIndeed,\n\\[\nX_i-2&lt;2s_{i+1}\\le X_i,\\qquad\n2s_{i+1}-1&lt;\\log(2^{2s_{i+1}}-1)&lt;2s_{i+1}.\n\\]</p><p>Inductively,\n\\[\nK_i\\ge256,\\qquad \\epsilon_iK_i\\ge32\\,2^{i-1},\\qquad\nK_{i+1}\\ge2^{K_i/3}\\ge K_i^2\\ge4K_i.\n\\]\nThe first two hold initially. If they hold at level \\(i\\), then \\(\\ell_i\\ge K_i^2\\) gives\n\\[\nX_i\\ge\\frac8{25}K_i2^{K_i}\\ge6,\\qquad\n\\ell_{i+1}\\ge\\frac4{25}K_i2^{K_i}\\ge2^{K_i}.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nK_{i+1}\\ge2^{K_i/2}-1\\ge2^{K_i/3}\\ge K_i^2\\ge4K_i.\n\\]\nThese inequalities hold for \\(K_i\\ge256\\); for example \\(K/3-2\\log K\\) is increasing and positive there. Since \\(\\epsilon_{i+1}=\\epsilon_i/2\\), the second bound propagates. Thus all parameters are defined, \\(H_i\\mid N_i\\), and \\(D_i^*\\le H_i\\).</p><p>Suppose \\(\\mathcal W_i\\) and its vector labeling are fixed. Generate a list \\((U_0,\\ldots,U_{N_i-1})\\) in independent groups of \\(H_i\\) positions. Within each group, proceed left to right, choosing each label uniformly among nonzero vectors orthogonal to all earlier labels at allowed lags \\(2^k\\) remaining within that group. There are at most \\(\\lfloor K_i/8\\rfloor+1\\le K_i\\) linear equations. Their common kernel has codimension at most \\(K_i\\), whether or not the equations are independent. Since \\(2s_i\\ge\\ell_i\\ge K_i^2\\), there are at least\n\\[\n2^{2s_i-K_i}-1\\ge2^{2s_i-K_i-1}\\ge\\frac{L_i}{2^{2K_i}}\n\\]\nnonzero choices.</p><p>Call this law \\(P_i^0\\). Every sampled list satisfies \\(\\mathrm{(W1)}\\). Put\n\\[\n\\zeta_i=2^{2K_i}/L_i.\n\\]\nThe conditional probability of a specified label at any position, given all preceding positions, is at most \\(\\zeta_i\\). Conditioning successively backward through specified positions gives\n\\[\nP_i^0\\{U_{r_j}=v_j\\text{ for all }j\\}\\le\\zeta_i^{d'}.\n\\]\nUnspecified labels are integrated out; independence within a group is not required.</p><p>Each \\(U_r\\) nevertheless has a uniform marginal on \\(\\mathcal W_i\\). The group preserving the alternating form acts transitively on nonzero vectors. Given \\(v\\ne0\\), nondegeneracy supplies \\(w\\) with \\([v,w]_i=1\\). Their plane is nondegenerate, and its orthogonal complement is nondegenerate of dimension two less. Induction extends \\(v,w\\) to a symplectic basis. Mapping such bases proves transitivity. The sequential rule, including uniform choices, is equivariant, so each marginal is uniform.</p><p>For \\(i\\ge2\\), define\n\\[\nf_t(u)=c_{i-1}(u)[t,t+D_{i-1}^*),\\qquad\n0\\le t\\le N_{i-1}-D_{i-1}^*,\n\\]\nand let \\(p_t\\) be its law for uniform \\(u\\in\\mathcal W_i\\). For each of the \\(q_i=N_i/H_i\\) independent groups, form the empirical probability vector of its \\(H_i\\) values \\(f_t(U_r)\\). Its mean is \\(p_t\\). Their average is the law of \\(f_t(U_J)\\) for uniform \\(J\\) on the sampled list. The alphabet has cardinality at most \\(L_{i-1}^{D_{i-1}^*}\\). Lemma 6.1, Markov’s inequality, and a union over at most \\(N_{i-1}\\) offsets bound failure of any \\(\\mathrm{(W4)}\\) requirement by\n\\[\nF_i=\\vartheta^{-1}N_{i-1}\n\\sqrt{\\frac{L_{i-1}^{D_{i-1}^*}}{N_i/H_i}}.\n\\]\nBelow, \\(F_i&lt;1/2\\) is verified.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal E_i\\) be the event that all these requirements hold, and put\n\\[\nP_i=P_i^0(\\,\\cdot\\,|\\mathcal E_i).\n\\]\nFor \\(i=1\\), take the whole sample space. Since \\(P_i^0(\\mathcal E_i)\\ge1/2\\),\n\\[\nP_i\\{U_{r_j}=v_j\\text{ for all }j\\}\\le2\\zeta_i^{d'}.\n\\]\nThe factor two is paid once for the conditioning event. No uniform marginal or group independence is asserted for \\(P_i\\). Its support satisfies \\(\\mathrm{(W1)}\\) and the per-list condition \\(\\mathrm{(W4)}\\).</p><p>Set \\(M_i=L_{i+1}\\), and sample \\(M_i\\) independent complete lists with law \\(P_i\\). For each offset \\(t\\), let \\(Q_{i,t}\\) be the length-\\(D_i^*\\) window law under \\(P_i\\). It satisfies the specified-coordinate bound. The empirical window law is an average of independent point-mass vectors on an alphabet of size \\(L_i^{D_i^*}\\). Lemma 6.1, Markov’s inequality, and a union over at most \\(N_i\\) offsets bound window-approximation failure by\n\\[\nE_i=\\vartheta^{-1}N_i\\sqrt{\\frac{L_i^{D_i^*}}{L_{i+1}}}.\n\\]</p><p>To impose distances, first use the reference law under which labels in each distinct indexed list are independent and uniform. Consider a pairwise comparison of distinct indices, or a shifted comparison of a target list with a window from two source lists. In the shifted case the three indices may coincide.</p><p>For a specified set of matching positions, form a multigraph with vertices \\((\\text{sample index},\\text{position})\\) and equality edges. Every target position occurs once. Source positions also occur once: they use \\(t,\\ldots,N_i-1\\) in one source and \\(0,\\ldots,t-1\\) in the other, disjoint ranges even when the source indices agree. The degree is at most two, counting repeated edges. There is no loop: pairwise indices are distinct, and a shifted source position differs from its target position by \\(t\\) modulo \\(N_i\\).</p><p>A connected component with \\(v\\) vertices has probability \\(L_i^{-(v-1)}\\). Paths have one fewer edge than vertices; cycles have equal numbers, with at least two edges. Every component therefore imposes at least half as many independent equalities as edges, including a pair of parallel edges. If \\(e\\) matches are specified, their probability is at most \\(L_i^{-e/2}\\). Distance failure entails at least \\(\\lceil\\epsilon_iN_i\\rceil\\) matching positions. Summing over at most \\(2^{N_i}\\) sets gives\n\\[\n2^{N_i}L_i^{-\\epsilon_iN_i/2}.\n\\]</p><p>A complete list under \\(P_i^0\\) has likelihood ratio at most \\(2^{2K_iN_i}\\) relative to the reference law, and under \\(P_i\\) at most \\(2\\,2^{2K_iN_i}\\). Each comparison uses at most three distinct sampled lists; repeated indices do not add an independent list. There are fewer than \\(4N_iM_i^3\\) comparisons. Their union failure probability is at most\n\\[\nT_i=32N_iL_{i+1}^3\\,2^{(6K_i+1)N_i}L_i^{-\\epsilon_iN_i/2}.\n\\]</p><p>It remains to bound these three probabilities uniformly. For \\(K\\ge256\\), put\n\\[\nB_0(K)=14+K+\\frac12\\,2^{K/8}(K+1)^2.\n\\]\nThe inequalities\n\\[\n14+K\\le2^{K/8},\\qquad (K+1)^2\\le2^{K/8}\n\\]\nhold at \\(256\\) and persist as the relevant ratios decrease. Hence\n\\[\nB_0(K)\\le2^{K/4}\\le\\frac18\\,2^{K/3}.\n\\]\nAlso \\(\\log(\\vartheta^{-1})&lt;14\\) and \\(K_i^2\\le\\ell_i&lt;(K_i+1)^2\\).</p><p>For \\(F_i\\), put \\(K=K_{i-1}\\) and use \\(\\log(N_i/H_i)\\ge K_i/2\\):\n\\[\n\\log F_i\\le B_0(K)-K_i/4\\le-\\frac18\\,2^{K/3}.\n\\]\nFor \\(E_i\\), put \\(K=K_i\\), and use \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\ge2^K\\):\n\\[\n\\log E_i\\le B_0(K)-\\ell_{i+1}/2\n\\le2^{K/4}-2^K/2\\le-2^K/4.\n\\]\nFinally, the rate bound and\n\\[\n\\epsilon_i\\ell_i\\ge\\epsilon_iK_i^2\\ge32K_i\n\\]\ngive\n\\[\n\\log T_i\n\\le5+K_i+N_i\\left(6K_i+1-\\frac{47}{100}\\epsilon_i\\ell_i\\right)\n\\le5+K_i-9K_i2^{K_i}\n\\le-K_i2^{K_i}.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nF_i\\le2^{-2^{K_{i-1}/3}/8}&lt;1/2,\\qquad\nE_i\\le2^{-2^{K_i}/4}&lt;1/4,\\qquad\nT_i\\le2^{-K_i2^{K_i}}&lt;1/4.\n\\]</p><p>The conditioning event has positive probability, and the two later failures have total probability less than \\(1/2\\). Therefore a tuple satisfying every requirement exists. The pairwise distance requirement for distinct sample indices has positive lower bound, so no sampled words coincide. The tuple has exactly \\(L_{i+1}\\) distinct members. Flatten their constituent lists to form \\(\\mathcal W_{i+1}\\). Its empirical distribution is exactly the uniform distribution on this set, without deduplication or changed weights. Choose its bijection with \\(\\mathcal A_{i+1}\\) and continue.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, “Averages of probability vectors.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(Y_1,\\ldots,Y_q\\) be independent random probability vectors on a set of cardinality \\(A\\), all with mean \\(p\\). Coordinates within an individual vector need not be independent.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\mathbb E\\operatorname{TV}\\!\\left(\\frac1q\\sum_{b=1}^qY_b,p\\right)\n\\le\\frac12\\sqrt{\\frac Aq}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(\\widehat p=q^{-1}\\sum_bY_b\\). Independence between vectors gives\n\\[\n\\sum_a\\operatorname{Var}(\\widehat p(a))\n=\\frac1{q^2}\\sum_{b,a}\\operatorname{Var}(Y_b(a))\n\\le\\frac1{q^2}\\sum_b\\mathbb E\\sum_aY_b(a)^2\n\\le\\frac1q.\n\\]\nApply \\(\\mathbb E|Z|\\le(\\mathbb E|Z|^2)^{1/2}\\) coordinatewise, then Cauchy–Schwarz to the sum over the \\(A\\) coordinates.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Lemma 5.3, “Recognition of words and alignment.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the families and parameters of Proposition 6. Define\n\\[\nd_i=\\prod_{j=1}^{i-1}(1-\\epsilon_j),\\qquad\nd_*=\\prod_{j=1}^{\\infty}(1-\\epsilon_j)&gt;0.\n\\]\nDistances below count symbols in the fixed alphabet \\(\\mathcal A\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Distinct \\(v,w\\in\\mathcal W_i\\) satisfy\n\\[\nd_H(v,w)\\ge d_im_i\\ge d_*m_i.\n\\]\nFor all \\(v,w,z\\in\\mathcal W_i\\), allowing repeated source words, and every \\(1\\le t&lt;m_i\\),\n\\[\nd_H((vw)[t,t+m_i),z)\\ge d_im_i\\ge d_*m_i.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Finite products are bounded below by\n\\[\n1-\\sum_{j\\ge1}\\epsilon_j=3/4,\n\\]\nso \\(d_*&gt;0\\). At level one, distinct one-symbol words have distance one and the offset assertion is vacuous.</p><p>Suppose both assertions hold at level \\(i\\). Distinct level-\\((i+1)\\) words differ in at least \\((1-\\epsilon_i)N_i\\) constituent labels by \\(\\mathrm{(W2)}\\). Each unequal constituent pair contributes at least \\(d_im_i\\) symbol mismatches. Thus their distance is at least \\(d_{i+1}m_{i+1}\\).</p><p>Consider a nonzero offset \\(t&lt;m_{i+1}\\) in two concatenated level-\\((i+1)\\) words compared with a third. If \\(m_i\\mid t\\), then \\(1\\le t/m_i&lt;N_i\\). The constituent offset requirement gives at least \\((1-\\epsilon_i)N_i\\) unequal pairs, yielding the same bound.</p><p>If \\(m_i\\nmid t\\), each of the \\(N_i\\) aligned target constituents faces a length-\\(m_i\\) source window at the same nonzero offset modulo \\(m_i\\). This window crosses two consecutive legal level-\\(i\\) words. The inductive offset assertion gives at least \\(d_im_i\\) mismatches on each target constituent, including when the source constituents cross the join of the larger words. Summing over disjoint target constituents gives\n\\[\nd_im_{i+1}\\ge d_{i+1}m_{i+1}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Lemma 5.4, “Word rates.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the fixed parameters of Proposition 6, and put\n\\[\nR_i=\\ell_i/m_i.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(m_i\\) is a power of two, with\n\\[\n\\log m_i=\\sum_{j&lt;i}K_j.\n\\]\nThe rates satisfy\n\\[\nR_{i+1}\\le\\frac{\\epsilon_i}{100}R_i,\\qquad R_i\\to0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nR_i\\ge R_1\\,200^{-(i-1)}2^{-(i-1)(i+4)/2}\n\\ge R_1\\,2^{-(i-1)(i+20)/2}.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{\\infty}K_iR_i^{16}=\\infty.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The formula for \\(m_i\\) follows from \\(m_1=1\\) and \\(N_i=2^{K_i}\\). Dividing the bounds for \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\) in Proposition 6’s construction by \\(m_{i+1}=N_im_i\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{\\epsilon_i}{200}R_i\\le R_{i+1}\\le\\frac{\\epsilon_i}{100}R_i.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\epsilon_i\\le1/8\\),\n\\[\nR_i\\le800^{-(i-1)}R_1\\to0.\n\\]\nIteration of the lower bound and\n\\[\n\\sum_{j&lt;i}(j+2)=(i-1)(i+4)/2\n\\]\ngive its first stated lower bound. The second uses \\(\\log200&lt;8\\).</p><p>The growth \\(K_{i+1}\\ge K_i^2\\) gives \\(K_i\\ge K_1^{2^{i-1}}\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\log(K_iR_i^{16})\n\\ge2^{i-1}\\log K_1+16\\log R_1-8(i-1)(i+20)\n\\longrightarrow\\infty.\n\\]\nThe summands do not tend to zero, so their series diverges.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 6.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the word families of Proposition 6. Let\n\\[\n\\Omega=\\mathcal A^{\\mathbb Z}\\times\\prod_{i\\ge1}\\mathbb Z/m_i\\mathbb Z\n\\]\nwith its compact product topology. Write points as \\((\\omega,a)\\), with \\(a_i\\in\\{0,\\ldots,m_i-1\\}\\).</p><p>Let \\(X\\subset\\Omega\\) consist of the points satisfying:\n\\[\na_{i+1}\\equiv a_i\\pmod{m_i}\n\\]\nfor every \\(i\\), and, whenever \\(t\\equiv-a_i\\pmod{m_i}\\),\n\\[\n(\\omega_t,\\ldots,\\omega_{t+m_i-1})\\in\\mathcal W_i.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nT(\\omega,a)=(\\omega',a'),\\qquad\n\\omega_t'=\\omega_{t+1},\\qquad\na_i'=a_i+1\\pmod{m_i}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The space \\(X\\) is nonempty and compact, and \\(T\\) is a homeomorphism of \\(X\\). There exists a \\(T\\)-invariant ergodic Borel probability \\(\\mu\\) on \\(X\\). Every \\(T\\)-invariant Borel probability on \\(X\\) is nonatomic, is standard modulo null sets, and has zero Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy.</p>", "proof": "<p>The conditions defining \\(X\\) are closed, and \\(T\\) is a homeomorphism of \\(X\\). For each \\(j\\), repeat a word of \\(\\mathcal W_j\\) bi-infinitely with level-\\(j\\) boundary at zero and zero phases. Concatenation compatibility makes it satisfy all conditions through level \\(j\\). The finite-level closed sets are nested and nonempty, so compactness gives \\(X\\ne\\varnothing\\).</p><p>For \\(x\\in X\\), weak limits of\n\\[\n\\frac1n\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\\delta_{T^jx}\n\\]\nare invariant: the difference of integrals of \\(f\\circ T\\) and \\(f\\) tends to zero for every continuous \\(f\\). The invariant-probability set \\(\\mathcal M_T(X)\\) is therefore nonempty, compact, and convex.</p><p>Choose a countable uniformly dense family \\((f_j)\\) of continuous real functions. Successively restrict \\(\\mathcal M_T(X)\\) to the compact face maximizing \\(\\int f_j\\,d\\mu\\). The nested intersection is nonempty and consists of one measure, since all these integrals determine it. An intersection of faces is a face, so this measure is extreme. A \\(T\\)-invariant measurable set of mass strictly between zero and one would give a nontrivial convex decomposition by normalized restrictions. Hence the measure is ergodic. The same applies to invariant sets modulo null sets after choosing Borel representatives.</p><p>For any invariant probability, \\(a_i\\) is uniform on \\(\\mathbb Z/m_i\\mathbb Z\\), because \\(T\\) cyclically permutes its level sets. A measurable atom of mass \\(c&gt;0\\) would be contained modulo null sets in one such level set for every \\(i\\), giving \\(c\\le1/m_i\\) for all \\(i\\), impossible as \\(m_i\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>The space \\(X\\) is compact metrizable, being a subspace of a countable product of finite discrete sets. To establish the stated standardness directly, encode the coordinates into successive disjoint ternary-digit blocks using digits \\(0,2\\). This gives a continuous injection into a compact subset of \\([0,1]\\). Let \\(\\lambda\\) be the image law and\n\\[\nF(t)=\\lambda([0,t])\n\\]\nits continuous distribution function. Set\n\\[\nQ(u)=\\inf\\{t:F(t)\\ge u\\},\\qquad 0&lt;u&lt;1.\n\\]\nContinuity gives \\(F(Q(u))=u\\), and \\(\\{u:Q(u)\\le t\\}\\) has Lebesgue measure \\(F(t)\\). Thus\n\\[\nQ_*\\operatorname{Leb}=\\lambda,\\qquad F_*\\lambda=\\operatorname{Leb}.\n\\]\nMoreover \\(Q(F(x))\\le x\\) almost surely, and both sides have law \\(\\lambda\\). Their nonnegative difference has zero integral, so equality holds almost surely. On measurable conull sets these maps are inverse isomorphisms. Compose with the coordinate embedding. Completion changes nothing.</p><p>For entropy, a finite-coordinate partition is refined by one recording \\(\\omega_{-r},\\ldots,\\omega_r\\) and \\(a_I\\), for fixed \\(r,I\\). Fix \\(j\\ge I\\). The initial phase \\(a_j\\) determines \\(a_I\\), all level-\\(j\\) boundaries, and the subsequent phase evolution. The symbol interval \\([-r,n-1+r]\\) used by an \\(n\\)-step name meets at most\n\\[\n\\left\\lceil\\frac{n+2r}{m_j}\\right\\rceil+1\n\\]\nlevel-\\(j\\) words. Thus the number of names is at most\n\\[\nm_jL_j^{\\lceil(n+2r)/m_j\\rceil+1}.\n\\]\nFirst hold \\(j\\) fixed and let \\(n\\to\\infty\\); the entropy rate is at most \\(R_j\\). Then let \\(j\\to\\infty\\) and use Proposition 8. Every finite-coordinate partition has entropy rate zero, without any frequency assumption.</p><p>For an arbitrary finite measurable partition \\(\\mathcal P\\) with \\(A\\) atoms, the cylinder algebra is dense in measure. Approximate its label by an \\(A\\)-valued finite-coordinate label \\(\\mathcal Q\\) with mismatch probability \\(s\\) arbitrarily small. The chain rule gives\n\\[\nH(\\mathcal P^n)\n\\le H(\\mathcal Q^n)+\\sum_{t=0}^{n-1}\nH(T^{-t}\\mathcal P\\mid T^{-t}\\mathcal Q)\n=H(\\mathcal Q^n)+nH(\\mathcal P\\mid\\mathcal Q).\n\\]\nRecording whether the labels differ gives\n\\[\nH(\\mathcal P\\mid\\mathcal Q)\\le H_{\\mathrm{bin}}(s)+s\\log A.\n\\]\nEntropy rates exist by subadditivity. Divide by \\(n\\), pass to the limit, then let \\(s\\downarrow0\\). This proves \\(h_\\mu(T)=0\\) for every invariant probability.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Lemma 6.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(\\mu\\) be any invariant probability on the space \\(X\\) of Proposition 9. For \\(x=(\\omega,a)\\), let \\(V_i(x)\\in\\mathcal W_i\\) be the word containing the origin, beginning at \\(-a_i(x)\\). Put\n\\[\nA_i=\\{x:a_i(x)=0\\},\\qquad \\mu_i=m_i\\mu|_{A_i}.\n\\]\nFor an integer \\(t\\), define\n\\[\nr_i(x,t)=(-a_i(T^tx))\\bmod m_i\\in\\{0,\\ldots,m_i-1\\}.\n\\]\nFor \\(D\\ge2\\), let\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{i,D}(x,t)\n=\\bigl(V_i(T^{t+r_i(x,t)+jm_i}x)\\bigr)_{j=0}^{D-2}.\n\\]\nIts \\(D-1\\) words lie in \\([t,t+Dm_i)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The law of \\(\\mathcal L_{i,D}(\\cdot,t)\\) under \\(\\mu\\) is the law under \\(\\mu_i\\) of the first \\(D-1\\) level-\\(i\\) words beginning at zero.</p><p>Under \\(\\mu_i\\), the index\n\\[\nJ=a_{i+2}/m_i\n\\]\nis uniform on \\(\\{0,\\ldots,N_iN_{i+1}-1\\}\\) and independent of \\(V_{i+2}\\). More precisely,\n\\[\n\\mu_i(J=j,V_{i+2}=v)\n=\\frac{\\mu_{i+2}(V_{i+2}=v)}{N_iN_{i+1}}\n\\]\nfor every such \\(j\\) and \\(v\\in\\mathcal W_{i+2}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Invariance first reduces to \\(t=0\\). On the phase class \\(a_i=r\\), the map\n\\[\nT^{(-r)\\bmod m_i}\n\\]\nsends the normalized restriction of \\(\\mu\\) bijectively and measure-preservingly onto \\(\\mu_i\\). Apply this separately to all \\(m_i\\) phase classes to obtain the first assertion.</p><p>For the second, \\(T^{jm_i}\\) maps\n\\[\n\\{a_{i+2}=0,V_{i+2}=v\\}\n\\]\nonto\n\\[\n\\{a_{i+2}=jm_i,V_{i+2}=v\\}.\n\\]\nIt does not change the containing parent word because \\(0\\le jm_i&lt;m_{i+2}\\). These sets have equal \\(\\mu\\)-mass. Multiplication by \\(m_i\\), together with \\(m_{i+2}/m_i=N_iN_{i+1}\\), proves the formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition 6.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the word families of Proposition 6, the system of Proposition 9, any invariant probability \\(\\mu\\), and the lists \\(\\mathcal L_{i,D}\\) defined in Proposition 10.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently large \\(i\\), every power of two \\(2\\le D\\le D_i^*\\), and every integer \\(t\\),\n\\[\nH_\\mu(\\mathcal L_{i,D}(\\cdot,t))\n\\ge .996(D-1)\\ell_i.\n\\]\nThe large-\\(i\\) threshold is independent of \\(D,t\\) and of the invariant probability.</p>", "proof": "<p>Two elementary entropy facts are used. A law whose atoms are at most \\(M\\) has entropy at least \\(\\log(1/M)\\). If laws \\(P,Q\\) on an \\(A\\)-point set satisfy \\(\\operatorname{TV}(P,Q)\\le s\\), then\n\\[\nH(P)\\ge H(Q)-1-s\\log A.\n\\]\nIndeed, couple their labels with mismatch probability at most \\(s\\). The mismatch flag and chain rule bound either conditional entropy by \\(1+s\\log A\\).</p><p>Put \\(q=D-1\\). By Proposition 10 work under \\(\\mu_i\\), and decompose the uniform parent-slot index as\n\\[\nJ=hN_i+u,\\qquad 0\\le h&lt;N_{i+1},\\quad 0\\le u&lt;N_i.\n\\]\nConditional on \\(v=V_{i+2}\\) and \\(u\\), the slot \\(h\\) is uniform among the \\(N_{i+1}\\) constituent \\(\\mathcal W_{i+1}\\)-words of \\(v\\). Call \\(u\\) admissible if \\(u+D_i^*\\le N_i\\). Failure has probability\n\\[\n\\beta_i=\\frac{D_i^*-1}{N_i}\\le\\frac{D_i^*}{N_i}.\n\\]</p><p>For fixed \\(v\\) and admissible \\(u\\), property \\(\\mathrm{(W4)}\\) at level \\(i+1\\) puts the law of the \\(D_i^*\\) labels within \\(10^{-4}\\) of its law in a uniform member of \\(\\mathcal W_{i+1}\\). Property \\(\\mathrm{(W3)}\\) at level \\(i\\) puts that law within another \\(10^{-4}\\) of a law whose every specified \\(q\\)-coordinate atom is at most\n\\[\nM_i=2\\left(\\frac{2^{2K_i}}{L_i}\\right)^q.\n\\]\nRestriction to the first \\(q\\) entries does not increase total variation. The comparison law can depend on \\(u\\), but its atom bound does not.</p><p>Using \\(s=2\\cdot10^{-4}\\) and alphabet size \\(L_i^q\\), the conditional actual list entropy is at least\n\\[\nq\\ell_i-2K_iq-1-(1+sq\\ell_i)\n=q((1-s)\\ell_i-2K_i)-2.\n\\]\nBy concavity, mixing over \\(v\\) and admissible \\(u\\) preserves the bound. Nonadmissible offsets lose at most their fraction because entropy is nonnegative. Hence\n\\[\nH(\\mathcal L_{i,D})\n\\ge(1-\\beta_i)\\{q((1-s)\\ell_i-2K_i)-2\\}.\n\\]\nNow\n\\[\n\\beta_i\\to0,\\qquad 2K_i/\\ell_i\\to0,\\qquad 2/\\ell_i\\to0.\n\\]\nOnce each is at most \\(.0005\\), division by \\(q\\ell_i\\), using \\(q\\ge1\\), gives a coefficient at least\n\\[\n(1-.0005)(1-.0002-.0005-.0005)&gt;.996.\n\\]\nOnly the two indicated construction levels enter; errors do not accumulate through ancestors.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Lemma 6.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the system and list notation of Propositions 9 and 10, with any invariant probability \\(\\mu\\). Labels \\(u,v\\in\\mathcal W_i\\) pass the graph test when \\([u,v]_i=0\\). Let \\(2\\le D\\le D_i^*\\) be a power of two, and put \\(n=m_iD\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently large \\(i\\), with probability at least\n\\[\n1-\\alpha_{i,D},\\qquad \\alpha_{i,D}=\\frac{2D-2}{H_i},\n\\]\nall corresponding entries of\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{i,D}(x,-n),\\qquad \\mathcal L_{i,D}(x,0)\n\\]\npass the level-\\(i\\) graph test simultaneously. In particular,\n\\[\n\\sup_{D\\le D_i^*}\\alpha_{i,D}\\to0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(n\\) is a multiple of \\(m_i\\), both windows have the same alignment advance, say \\(q_0\\). Their entry starts are\n\\[\n-n+q_0+jm_i,\\qquad q_0+jm_i,\\qquad 0\\le j\\le D-2.\n\\]\nCorresponding entries are separated by exactly \\(D\\) level-\\(i\\) positions. From first to last start, the span has \\(2D-1\\) block positions, including the unused intervening block.</p><p>By Proposition 10, the first boundary has the boundary-start law. Its position modulo \\(H_i\\) is uniform: its position in a \\(\\mathcal W_{i+1}\\)-parent is uniform modulo \\(N_i\\), and \\(H_i\\mid N_i\\). Once \\(2D-1\\le H_i\\), exactly \\(2D-2\\) residues have a span crossing an \\(H_i\\)-group boundary. Parent boundaries are themselves group boundaries.</p><p>For every other residue, all labels belong to one prescribed group, and \\(\\mathrm{(W1)}\\) applies because \\(D\\) is an allowed power-of-two lag. Finally,\n\\[\n2D_i^*/H_i\\to0\n\\]\nby the parameter definitions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Lemma 7.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the word parameters of Proposition 6 and rates \\(R_i=\\ell_i/m_i\\). Let \\((B_k)_{k\\ge1}\\) be any positive sequence satisfying\n\\[\n\\sum_kB_k^{16}&lt;\\infty.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are levels \\(i\\to\\infty\\) and powers of two \\(D\\) with\n\\[\n2^{\\lceil K_i/16\\rceil}\\le D\\le2^{\\lfloor K_i/8\\rfloor}\n\\]\nsuch that, for \\(n=m_iD\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{B_{\\log n}}{R_i}\\to0.\n\\]\nConsequently, any partitions satisfying\n\\[\n\\log\\#\\mathcal D_k\\le CB_k2^k\n\\]\nobey\n\\[\n\\log\\#\\mathcal D_{\\log n}=o((D-1)\\ell_i)\n\\]\nalong this sequence, for every fixed \\(C\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(m_i\\) is a power of two, put\n\\[\nb_i'=\\log m_i,\\qquad b_{i+1}'=b_i'+K_i.\n\\]\nThe integer sets\n\\[\nJ_i=\\{b_i'+j:\\lceil K_i/16\\rceil\\le j\\le\\lfloor K_i/8\\rfloor\\}\n\\]\nare disjoint and, for large \\(i\\), contain at least \\(K_i/32\\) integers.</p><p>If the ratios were bounded below by \\(c&gt;0\\) on all sufficiently late tests, then Proposition 8 would give\n\\[\n\\sum_kB_k^{16}\n\\ge c^{16}\\sum_{i\\text{ large}}|J_i|R_i^{16}\n\\ge\\frac{c^{16}}{32}\\sum_{i\\text{ large}}K_iR_i^{16}\n=\\infty,\n\\]\na contradiction. Thus successively later tests can be selected with ratio tending to zero.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\nB_{\\log n}n=\\frac{B_{\\log n}}{R_i}D\\ell_i,\\qquad\n\\frac D{D-1}\\le2,\n\\]\nwhich proves the partition-cost assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Section 7, the obstruction to a smooth volume model", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the word families of Proposition 6 and an ergodic invariant probability \\(\\mu\\) on the system of Proposition 9.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose, for a contradiction, that this system has a smooth positive-volume model \\((M,\\nu,S)\\) as defined in Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Its dimension is positive, because a compact zero-dimensional manifold is finite and cannot carry a nonatomic probability.</p><p class=\"li\">Apply Proposition 5 with \\(\\eta=.01\\), obtaining partitions, original cell-weighted pair laws \\(\\rho\\), retained sets \\(G\\), and switches \\(z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Apply Proposition 13 to that model’s fixed sequence \\((B_k)\\). Let \\(i,D,n=m_iD\\) run through the selected tests, sufficiently late for all eventual estimates.</p><p class=\"li\">Transport symbolic observations to \\(M\\) by the measurable conjugacy on an invariant conull set and extend them arbitrarily on its null complement.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C\\) be the sampled cell. Conditional on \\(C\\), the input points \\(x,y\\) are independent, with normalized volume on that cell; both original marginals are \\(\\nu\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  U=\\mathcal L_{i,D}(y,-n),\\qquad\n  V=\\mathcal L_{i,D}(x,0),\\qquad q=D-1,\n  \\]\n  each list using its own input’s alignment, and define on the original pair space\n  \\[\n  Z(x,y)=\\frac1q\\sum_{j=0}^{q-1}\n  \\mathbf1_{\\{[U_j,V_j]_i=0\\}}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently late selected tests, the same original pair law satisfies both\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\rho Z\\le .62\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_\\rho Z\\ge .912.\n\\]\nConsequently, the fixed system has no smooth positive-volume model.</p>", "proof": "<p>The symbolic system and ergodic measure were fixed before proposing a model. A hypothetical smooth model fixes its dimension, all smooth constants, and the sequence supplied by Proposition 5. Proposition 13 supplies arbitrarily late tests at which conditioning on the smooth cell loses \\(o((D-1)\\ell_i)\\) bits.</p><p>Lemma 14.2 proves \\(\\mathbb E_\\rho Z\\le .62\\) for the original pair law. Lemmas 14.3 and 14.4 prove \\(\\mathbb E_\\rho Z\\ge .912\\) for that same law and same statistic. These inequalities contradict each other. Therefore the fixed system has no smooth positive-volume model.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(L=2^{2s}-1\\), and equip the nonzero vectors of \\(\\mathbb F_2^{2s}\\) with a nondegenerate alternating form. Let pairs of laws \\((p_\\xi,q_\\xi)\\) be indexed by a probability space, with\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\xi H(p_\\xi)\\ge .99\\log L,\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_\\xi H(q_\\xi)\\ge .99\\log L.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently large \\(L\\), independent samples from \\(p_\\xi,q_\\xi\\), followed by averaging over \\(\\xi\\), pass \\([u,v]=0\\) with probability at most \\(.62\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a law \\(p\\), let\n\\[\nA_p=\\{u:p(u)&gt;L^{-.8}\\},\\qquad \\theta_p=p(A_p).\n\\]\nThe set \\(A_p\\) has at most \\(L^{.8}\\) elements. Splitting entropy according to this set gives\n\\[\nH(p)\\le1+.8\\theta_p\\log L+(1-\\theta_p)\\log L\n=1+(1-.2\\theta_p)\\log L.\n\\]\nThe formula includes empty sets with the zero-mass convention. The entropy hypotheses imply\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\xi\\theta_{p_\\xi},\\ \\mathbb E_\\xi\\theta_{q_\\xi}\n\\le .05+\\frac5{\\log L}\\le .055\n\\]\nonce \\(\\log L\\ge1000\\).</p><p>Let \\(A\\) be the matrix \\(A_{uv}=(-1)^{[u,v]}\\) on nonzero vectors. On the full vector space, distinct rows are orthogonal: if \\(u\\ne w\\), nondegeneracy makes \\(v\\mapsto[u+w,v]\\) a nonzero linear functional, so\n\\[\n\\sum_v(-1)^{[u+w,v]}=0.\n\\]\nEvery row has squared norm \\(L+1\\). The full operator norm is \\(\\sqrt{L+1}\\), and restriction to nonzero rows and columns has no larger norm.</p><p>The light subprobability \\(p^l=p\\mathbf1_{A_p^c}\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\|p^l\\|_2^2\\le L^{-.8}\\sum_up^l(u)\\le L^{-.8}.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\left|\\sum_{u,v}p^l(u)A_{uv}q^l(v)\\right|\n\\le\\sqrt{L+1}\\,L^{-.8}=:\\beta_L.\n\\]\nThe passing mass of the light product is at most \\((1+\\beta_L)/2\\). Pairs with a heavy coordinate have total mass at most \\(\\theta_p+\\theta_q\\). Averaging and taking \\(L\\) large enough that \\(\\beta_L\\le .02\\), the passing probability is at most\n\\[\n.055+.055+\\frac{1+.02}{2}=.62.\n\\]\nThe matrix includes diagonal entries, so automatic relations \\([u,u]=0\\) need no separate treatment.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.2", "orig": "Section 7.2, equations (7.5)–(7.6)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the hypothetical model, selected tests, cell variable \\(C\\), lists \\(U,V\\), and original pair statistic \\(Z\\) of Proposition 14.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently late tests,\n\\[\n\\frac1q\\sum_{j=0}^{q-1}H(U_j\\mid C)\\ge .99\\ell_i,\\qquad\n\\frac1q\\sum_{j=0}^{q-1}H(V_j\\mid C)\\ge .99\\ell_i,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\rho Z\\le .62.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The two original marginals and Proposition 11 give\n\\[\nH_\\rho(U),\\ H_\\rho(V)\\ge .996q\\ell_i.\n\\]\nConditioning either vector on its sampled cell loses at most\n\\[\nH(C)\\le\\log\\#\\mathcal D_{\\log n}=o(q\\ell_i)\n\\]\nby Proposition 13. Conditional subadditivity then gives the two average-coordinate entropy bounds.</p><p>Apply Lemma 14.1 with index \\(\\xi=(C,j)\\), assigning \\(j\\) uniform weight independently of the cell. For each fixed cell and fixed \\(j\\), \\(U_j\\) depends only on \\(y\\) and \\(V_j\\) only on \\(x\\), so their laws are independent. Independence is used conditional on the full index: first fix \\(j\\), use the conditional product law given \\(C\\), then average. No product assertion after mixing over a shared random \\(j\\) is needed. The conclusion is \\(\\mathbb E_\\rho Z\\le .62\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.3", "orig": "Lemma 7.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the hypothetical smooth model and switching experiment of Proposition 14. Let \\(d_*&gt;0\\) be the fixed symbol-distance constant of Proposition 7, and let\n\\[\nf:M\\to\\mathcal A\n\\]\nbe the transported symbol at time zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently large dyadic times in Proposition 5, retained pairs satisfying both\n\\[\n\\#\\{0\\le t&lt;n:f(S^tz)\\ne f(S^tx)\\}&lt;d_*n/100\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\#\\{-n\\le t&lt;0:f(S^tz)\\ne f(S^ty)\\}&lt;d_*n/100\n\\]\nhave original \\(\\rho\\)-mass at least \\(.96\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(\\kappa&lt;d_*/20000\\). Inner regularity gives disjoint compact cores\n\\[\nK_a\\subset f^{-1}(a),\\qquad a\\in\\mathcal A,\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n\\nu\\!\\left(M\\setminus\\bigcup_aK_a\\right)&lt;\\kappa.\n\\]\nMeasurable atoms may first be replaced by Borel representatives modulo null sets. There are finitely many cores, and distinct nonempty cores have positive separation. If only one is nonempty, the equal-symbol implication is automatic; otherwise use the positive minimum separation. Uniform shadowing is smaller than this separation at all large scales. Thus compared iterates lying in cores have equal symbols.</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\sigma=z_*(\\rho|_G).\n\\]\nProposition 5 gives \\(\\sigma\\le2\\nu\\), and for every integer \\(t\\),\n\\[\n(S^t)_*\\sigma\\le2(S^t)_*\\nu=2\\nu.\n\\]\nThe \\(x,y\\) time marginals under the original law are \\(\\nu\\). At each comparison time, the \\(\\rho\\)-mass within \\(G\\) where either the input or \\(z\\) misses the cores is at most\n\\[\n\\kappa+2\\kappa=3\\kappa.\n\\]\nThis applies on both orbit halves.</p><p>The integral over \\(G\\) of either mismatch fraction is at most \\(3\\kappa\\). Markov’s inequality at threshold \\(d_*/100\\) on each half bounds combined failure mass by\n\\[\n600\\kappa/d_*&lt;.03.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\rho(G)\\ge .99\\), simultaneous success has original mass at least \\(.96\\), without division by \\(\\rho(G)\\).</p><p>The input marginals and \\(\\sigma\\le2\\nu\\) give zero mass to the complement of the invariant conjugacy domain. Symbol strings and orbit identities used here are therefore valid outside a \\(\\rho\\)-null set.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.4", "orig": "Section 7.4, “Recognition preserves the original entry pairing.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the selected tests, original statistic \\(Z\\), and symbol-matching event of Proposition 14 and Lemma 14.3. Let \\(E_{i,D}\\subset X\\) be the group-boundary exception in Proposition 12, transported to \\(M\\), with\n\\[\n\\nu(E_{i,D})\\le\\alpha_{i,D}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On the symbol-matching event, the three level-\\(i\\) phases agree:\n\\[\na_i(z)=a_i(x)=a_i(y).\n\\]\nFewer than \\(D/100\\) entries in either original input list differ from the corresponding list of \\(z\\). For all sufficiently late tests,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\rho Z\\ge(.96-.01)\\cdot .96=.912.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose the phases of \\(z,x\\) modulo \\(m_i\\) differ. Consider the \\(D-1\\) full level-\\(i\\) blocks of \\(x\\) beginning at its first aligned boundary in \\([0,n)\\). On each such interval, \\(z\\)’s string is a length-\\(m_i\\) window at a nonzero offset across two consecutive legal level-\\(i\\) words. This remains true if the words are equal or extend beyond the observed window.</p><p>Proposition 7 gives at least \\(d_*m_i\\) mismatches on each of these disjoint intervals. Their total is at least\n\\[\nd_*(D-1)m_i\\ge d_*n/2,\n\\]\ncontrary to the future mismatch bound. The same argument on the past half gives phase agreement with \\(y\\). Since \\(n\\) is a multiple of \\(m_i\\), \\(a_i(S^{-n}u)=a_i(u)\\) on the conjugacy domain. Thus\n\\[\na_i(z)=a_i(x)=a_i(y).\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(q_0\\) be the common advance to alignment. The already defined entries \\(U_j,V_j\\) occupy, in the symbolic orbit of \\(z\\), positions\n\\[\n-n+q_0+jm_i,\\qquad q_0+jm_i,\\qquad 0\\le j\\le D-2.\n\\]\nTheir difference is exactly \\(n=Dm_i\\); the index \\(j\\) has not changed after switching.</p><p>If one entry has a different label from \\(z\\)’s corresponding word, Proposition 7’s distinct-word separation costs at least \\(d_*m_i\\) symbol mismatches. Hence fewer than \\(D/100\\) entries on each side differ.</p><p>Proposition 12 gives\n\\[\n\\alpha_{i,D}\\le2D_i^*/H_i\\to0\n\\]\nuniformly over selected tests. The output measure bound implies\n\\[\n\\rho\\{(x,y)\\in G:z(x,y)\\in E_{i,D}\\}\\le2\\alpha_{i,D}.\n\\]\nTake a late test with \\(2\\alpha_{i,D}\\le .01\\). On a subset of original mass at least \\(.96-.01\\), the mismatch bounds hold and every corresponding pair in the two \\(z\\)-lists passes the graph test. Removing fewer than \\(D/100\\) wrong entries on each side gives\n\\[\nZ(x,y)\\ge1-\\frac{2D}{100(D-1)}\\ge .96.\n\\]\nSince \\(Z\\ge0\\) elsewhere,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\rho Z\\ge(.96-.01)\\cdot .96=.912.\n\\]</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 3, "cite"], [1, 4, "cite"], [1, 5, "cite"], [1, 6, "cite"], [1, 7, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [3, 4, "cite"], [3, 5, "cite"], [3, 7, "cite"], [3, 8, "cite"], [3, 9, "cite"], [4, 7, "cite"], [5, 9, "cite"], [6, 0, "cite"], [6, 20, "cite"], [6, 23, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [7, 8, "cite"], [7, 9, "cite"], [7, 10, "cite"], [8, 6, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [8, 10, "cite"], [9, 6, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [10, 6, "cite"], [11, 0, "cite"], [11, 13, "cite"], [11, 14, "cite"], [11, 15, "cite"], [11, 17, "cite"], [11, 19, "cite"], [11, 20, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [13, 0, "cite"], [13, 23, "cite"], [13, 24, "cite"], [14, 0, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [14, 19, "cite"], [15, 0, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [15, 17, "cite"], [15, 18, "cite"], [15, 20, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [16, 18, "cite"], [17, 22, "cite"], [18, 24, "cite"], [19, 0, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [19, 22, "cite"], [20, 0, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [22, 20, "cite"], [23, 20, "cite"], [23, 24, "cite"], [24, 20, "cite"]]});
